1
00:00:21,167 --> 00:00:25,986
Here is a farmer. For four years running, his
farm has lost money.

2
00:00:26,433 --> 00:00:32,732
Every year, more money went out of the farm
than came in. And every year the hole got

3
00:00:32,732 --> 00:00:33,251
deeper.

4
00:00:33,700 --> 00:00:39,801
Anybody watching would tell him to stop. He
does not stop. He keeps farming.

5
00:00:43,333 --> 00:00:50,317
And here is Nell. Nell owns a small factory,
and she has the chance to build a second one.

6
00:00:50,767 --> 00:00:56,860
The sums say the new factory would earn her
far more than her money is costing her. On

7
00:00:56,860 --> 00:00:59,411
paper, she should build it tomorrow.

8
00:00:59,833 --> 00:01:04,866
She refuses. Not this year. Perhaps not next
year either.

9
00:01:07,700 --> 00:01:10,063
Both of them look irrational.

10
00:01:10,900 --> 00:01:14,760
Both of them are, in fact, behaving correctly.

11
00:01:15,600 --> 00:01:22,503
And by the end of this film, you will be able
to say exactly why — in your own words.

12
00:01:25,400 --> 00:01:32,928
Nell makes the decisions. She owns the
factory. Every choice in this film is hers, and she

13
00:01:32,928 --> 00:01:34,935
is sensible — not timid.

14
00:01:35,367 --> 00:01:41,856
Marshall carries the textbook. He always gives
the standard answer. He is right when the

15
00:01:41,856 --> 00:01:45,247
world is calm, and badly wrong when it is not.

16
00:01:45,667 --> 00:01:50,873
And Ava asks the questions you would ask. She
never lets Marshall get away with anything.

17
00:01:51,300 --> 00:01:57,828
We start from nothing at all. No economics is
assumed. Not one word of it.

18
00:02:00,667 --> 00:02:04,528
a farmer absorbs losses, and will not shut

19
00:02:04,667 --> 00:02:08,423
an owner refuses a project the sums approve

20
00:02:08,567 --> 00:02:11,353
both are correct — that is the film

21
00:02:19,766 --> 00:02:25,356
Start with the simplest picture there is. A
factory. Nell's factory.

22
00:02:25,799 --> 00:02:32,214
Every month, money comes in. Customers buy
what she makes, and they pay her.

23
00:02:34,333 --> 00:02:41,318
Economists call that revenue. All the money
coming in, before anything is taken out of it.

24
00:02:44,033 --> 00:02:52,041
Every month, money also goes out. She pays for
materials, for electricity, for wages, for

25
00:02:52,041 --> 00:02:52,851
the rent.

26
00:02:54,999 --> 00:02:59,115
cost. All the money going out to keep the
place running.

27
00:03:01,599 --> 00:03:06,121
The difference between the two is what she
actually keeps.

28
00:03:08,266 --> 00:03:12,426
profit. Revenue minus cost. What is left over.

29
00:03:16,633 --> 00:03:23,679
Not all costs behave the same way. The rent is
the same whether she makes one chair or a

30
00:03:23,679 --> 00:03:24,400
thousand.

31
00:03:26,533 --> 00:03:31,254
fixed cost. A cost that does not change with
how much you make.

32
00:03:33,599 --> 00:03:40,192
The wood is different. Make twice as many
chairs and she buys twice as much wood.

33
00:03:42,333 --> 00:03:47,747
variable cost. A cost that rises and falls
with how much you make.

34
00:03:52,366 --> 00:03:57,190
You may think investment means buying shares
in a company. That is not what an economist

35
00:03:57,190 --> 00:03:57,848
means by it.

36
00:03:58,266 --> 00:04:04,854
It means this. You spend a large sum of money
now, to build something that will produce

37
00:04:04,854 --> 00:04:05,914
revenue later.

38
00:04:08,033 --> 00:04:13,976
investment. Spending a large sum now to build
something that earns later.

39
00:04:17,066 --> 00:04:24,663
A factory. A shop fitted out. A well drilled.
A machine bought. That is investment, and that

40
00:04:24,663 --> 00:04:28,049
is the decision this whole film is about.

41
00:04:30,866 --> 00:04:34,204
revenue − cost = profit

42
00:04:34,333 --> 00:04:37,241
fixed stays put · variable moves

43
00:04:37,366 --> 00:04:41,401
investment = spend now, earn later

44
00:04:49,799 --> 00:04:56,473
Marshall has a rule. It is the rule in every
textbook, and it is worth stating fairly,

45
00:04:56,473 --> 00:05:02,293
because for most of the last century it was
simply what everybody believed.

46
00:05:02,733 --> 00:05:09,141
Take everything she spends that moves with
output — the wood, the power, the wages — and

47
00:05:09,141 --> 00:05:12,346
divide it by the number of chairs she makes.

48
00:05:14,466 --> 00:05:19,808
average variable cost. What each extra chair
costs her to make.

49
00:05:22,299 --> 00:05:28,789
Now add a share of the cost of the building
itself — the factory that had to be paid for

50
00:05:28,789 --> 00:05:31,075
before a single chair was made.

51
00:05:33,199 --> 00:05:38,714
long-run average cost. Everything a chair
costs, the building included.

52
00:05:42,899 --> 00:05:49,956
This is the most important picture in the
film, so we build it slowly. One line, running up

53
00:05:49,956 --> 00:05:56,314
the screen. Low down, the money coming in each
month is poor. High up, it is good.

54
00:05:58,133 --> 00:06:05,376
Marshall's first half. If the money coming in
clears the long-run average cost, then build.

55
00:06:05,376 --> 00:06:07,286
Expand. Enter the trade.

56
00:06:07,733 --> 00:06:14,694
Marshall's second half. If the money coming in
drops below the average variable cost — if a

57
00:06:14,694 --> 00:06:19,513
chair no longer covers even the wood and the
wages — then stop.

58
00:06:19,933 --> 00:06:26,735
Notice how close together those two lines are.
Between them is a narrow band where the right

59
00:06:26,735 --> 00:06:33,020
thing to do is nothing at all. Hold that
thought. It is going to get very much wider.

60
00:06:37,166 --> 00:06:44,295
One definition left, and it is the single most
important one in this film. Nell needs a

61
00:06:44,295 --> 00:06:50,031
large sum to build. It comes from one of two
places, and usually both.

62
00:06:50,466 --> 00:06:55,170
She borrows it, and then she has to pay extra
for the use of it.

63
00:06:55,599 --> 00:07:01,588
Or it is her own money, and by putting it into
this factory she gives up whatever else she

64
00:07:01,588 --> 00:07:03,185
could have done with it.

65
00:07:03,633 --> 00:07:09,622
Either way, the money has a price. Blend the
two together and you get one number.

66
00:07:11,766 --> 00:07:17,333
cost of capital. The yearly percentage her
money must earn to be worth using.

67
00:07:21,433 --> 00:07:27,767
Earn less than that, and she would have done
better leaving the money where it was. Every

68
00:07:27,767 --> 00:07:31,255
number later in this film is measured against
it.

69
00:07:34,099 --> 00:07:37,577
AVC: what one more chair costs

70
00:07:37,699 --> 00:07:40,526
LRAC: plus a share of the building

71
00:07:40,666 --> 00:07:44,097
build above LRAC · shut below AVC

72
00:07:44,233 --> 00:07:47,431
cost of capital: what money must earn

73
00:07:54,366 --> 00:08:00,578
Marshall's rule is clear, and it is wrong.
Here are three facts that do not fit it. All

74
00:08:00,578 --> 00:08:04,433
three come straight out of the paper we are
following.

75
00:08:08,033 --> 00:08:13,569
hurdle rate. The return a firm insists a
project must promise.

76
00:08:16,666 --> 00:08:19,818
Here is what a firm's money costs it.

77
00:08:20,266 --> 00:08:27,564
And here is what firms insist a project
promises before they will build it. Three or four

78
00:08:27,564 --> 00:08:28,712
times as much.

79
00:08:30,533 --> 00:08:37,515
Asked directly, firms named rates from eight
to thirty per cent. The middle answer was

80
00:08:37,515 --> 00:08:40,356
fifteen. The average was seventeen.

81
00:08:42,199 --> 00:08:49,049
And the safe cost of their money at the time?
A nominal rate of four per cent, and a real

82
00:08:49,049 --> 00:08:50,512
rate close to zero.

83
00:08:54,033 --> 00:09:01,108
Second. In the middle of the
nineteen-eighties, American farmers stayed open through long

84
00:09:01,108 --> 00:09:02,698
stretches of losses.

85
00:09:03,133 --> 00:09:09,780
In nineteen eighty-three the average net
income for a farm operator was six thousand

86
00:09:09,780 --> 00:09:11,521
dollars. For the year.

87
00:09:13,366 --> 00:09:20,454
Even leaving out the rent on the land and the
mortgage payments altogether, it only rises to

88
00:09:20,454 --> 00:09:25,693
thirteen and a half thousand. For a whole
family, for a year's work.

89
00:09:27,533 --> 00:09:31,589
Marshall's rule says: stop. They did not stop.

90
00:09:35,433 --> 00:09:40,551
exchange rate. How much of one country's money
another's will buy.

91
00:09:44,966 --> 00:09:48,711
imports. Goods made abroad and sold here.

92
00:09:52,133 --> 00:09:58,850
Here is the dollar. Up sharply to the end of
nineteen eighty-four — it rose by about fifty

93
00:09:58,850 --> 00:10:05,716
per cent — then falling from early nineteen
eighty-five, until by the end of eighty-seven it

94
00:10:05,716 --> 00:10:09,523
was almost back where it had been in
seventy-eight.

95
00:10:09,966 --> 00:10:17,301
And here are the imports. Flat for three years
while the dollar climbed. Then rising — and

96
00:10:17,301 --> 00:10:21,947
still rising, long after the reason for it had
gone away.

97
00:10:23,766 --> 00:10:30,464
The paper gives that level two different ways
on two different pages. We use the first.

98
00:10:33,999 --> 00:10:38,212
Firms turn down work that is profitable on
paper.

99
00:10:38,633 --> 00:10:42,807
Farmers absorb years of losses rather than
shut.

100
00:10:43,233 --> 00:10:48,103
And imports arrive years late, then refuse to
leave.

101
00:10:48,533 --> 00:10:52,920
They are not being stupid. The next four
chapters explain why.

102
00:10:55,766 --> 00:11:00,079
hurdle rates: 3–4× the cost of capital

103
00:11:00,199 --> 00:11:03,143
farmers absorbed years of losses

104
00:11:03,266 --> 00:11:06,465
imports arrived late — and stayed

105
00:11:13,399 --> 00:11:20,552
Everything that follows rests on three things
being true at once. Each one gets a picture,

106
00:11:20,552 --> 00:11:25,956
and each one gets an icon you will see again
whenever it is in play.

107
00:11:27,866 --> 00:11:34,840
Nell pays for the concrete foundation of her
new factory. The money leaves her hands, and

108
00:11:34,840 --> 00:11:36,173
the slab appears.

109
00:11:36,599 --> 00:11:44,275
Now she changes her mind, and tries to sell
the foundation back. Nobody wants a hole in a

110
00:11:44,275 --> 00:11:46,518
field with concrete in it.

111
00:11:48,666 --> 00:11:53,461
sunk cost. Money that cannot be got back if
you change your mind.

112
00:11:56,799 --> 00:12:03,976
And be careful. Sunk is not the same as
expensive. A lorry is expensive and you can sell it

113
00:12:03,976 --> 00:12:08,156
again. A hole in the ground is cheap, and you
cannot.

114
00:12:11,699 --> 00:12:18,612
Second ingredient. Nell does not know what
next year holds. Will there be customers? How

115
00:12:18,612 --> 00:12:20,183
many? At what price?

116
00:12:20,633 --> 00:12:27,138
But the fog thins. Every month that passes
tells her a little more than she knew the month

117
00:12:27,138 --> 00:12:27,644
before.

118
00:12:29,766 --> 00:12:35,865
ongoing uncertainty. You do not know what is
coming, and you learn a little at a time.

119
00:12:40,899 --> 00:12:47,839
Third. If she does not build this year, the
chance is generally still there next year. The

120
00:12:47,839 --> 00:12:49,073
door stays open.

121
00:12:49,499 --> 00:12:54,641
This year. Next year. The year after that.
Still open.

122
00:12:57,066 --> 00:13:02,972
the chance keeps. The opportunity does not
vanish if you do not take it today.

123
00:13:07,366 --> 00:13:13,999
Money you cannot get back. A future you learn
about slowly. And a chance that keeps.

124
00:13:14,433 --> 00:13:18,021
When all three are true, waiting is worth
money.

125
00:13:19,866 --> 00:13:26,389
And when any one of them is missing, it is
not. Remember that — in chapter twenty we take

126
00:13:26,389 --> 00:13:30,493
the third one away, and the whole conclusion
flips over.

127
00:13:33,333 --> 00:13:36,299
sunk cost: money you cannot get back

128
00:13:36,433 --> 00:13:39,190
a future you learn a bit at a time

129
00:13:39,333 --> 00:13:41,370
a chance that keeps

130
00:13:41,499 --> 00:13:44,628
all three → waiting pays

131
00:13:53,033 --> 00:13:59,870
Put Nell exactly on Marshall's line. Exactly
on it. The money coming in is precisely enough

132
00:13:59,870 --> 00:14:03,853
to cover everything, including the cost of her
money.

133
00:14:04,299 --> 00:14:10,839
Marshall says she should be indifferent.
Build, or do not build. It makes no difference

134
00:14:10,839 --> 00:14:11,666
either way.

135
00:14:12,099 --> 00:14:16,421
Hang on. There is a third thing she could do,
and neither of those is it.

136
00:14:18,066 --> 00:14:23,060
Give her one very simple plan. Wait a fixed
stretch of time.

137
00:14:23,499 --> 00:14:26,083
Then look at the money coming in.

138
00:14:26,499 --> 00:14:33,632
If it is above the line, build. If it is not,
never build. It is not even the best plan — it

139
00:14:33,632 --> 00:14:37,587
is just a plan, and it is enough to make the
point.

140
00:14:41,633 --> 00:14:49,307
Now watch what the future might do, starting
from that point. Here are five futures. Nobody

141
00:14:49,307 --> 00:14:53,777
knows which one happens. Some end high. Some
end low.

142
00:14:54,199 --> 00:15:01,419
In the two futures that end high, she looks,
she sees the money is well above the line, and

143
00:15:01,419 --> 00:15:03,164
she builds. She gains.

144
00:15:03,599 --> 00:15:09,590
In the two that end low, she looks, and she
does not build. She gains nothing.

145
00:15:10,033 --> 00:15:17,250
But look at that again, because everything
turns on it. She gains nothing — and she loses

146
00:15:17,250 --> 00:15:17,898
nothing.

147
00:15:21,433 --> 00:15:28,172
Two outcomes that pay something. Three that
pay nothing at all — but cost nothing either.

148
00:15:28,599 --> 00:15:35,315
Weigh each by how likely it is, and add them
up. The answer is bigger than nothing. It

149
00:15:35,315 --> 00:15:37,189
cannot be anything else.

150
00:15:40,033 --> 00:15:47,139
Here is the sentence this chapter exists for.
Waiting cuts off the bad half, and keeps the

151
00:15:47,139 --> 00:15:47,929
good half.

152
00:15:51,566 --> 00:15:56,111
That selective removal of risk is what waiting
is worth.

153
00:16:00,099 --> 00:16:06,825
This is the most counter-intuitive part of the
chapter, so I am going to say it twice. It

154
00:16:06,825 --> 00:16:10,528
has nothing whatsoever to do with disliking
risk.

155
00:16:12,366 --> 00:16:19,430
Nell is assumed not to mind risk at all. She
would take a fair gamble without blinking. And

156
00:16:19,430 --> 00:16:20,672
she still waits.

157
00:16:22,499 --> 00:16:30,335
The trade she is making is between risk now
and risk later. It is about timing, not nerves.

158
00:16:33,166 --> 00:16:36,295
start exactly on the textbook's line

159
00:16:36,433 --> 00:16:39,840
good futures pay · bad ones cost nothing

160
00:16:39,966 --> 00:16:43,896
gains + zeros > nothing, so waiting wins

161
00:16:44,033 --> 00:16:47,429
and none of it is about disliking risk

162
00:16:55,833 --> 00:17:00,838
If waiting is worth money, why does she ever
build at all? Why not wait for ever?

163
00:17:01,266 --> 00:17:07,858
Because waiting is not free. Every month she
waits, she gives up a month of profit she could

164
00:17:07,858 --> 00:17:09,076
have been making.

165
00:17:12,699 --> 00:17:18,315
the trigger. The level the money coming in
must reach before building is right.

166
00:17:23,833 --> 00:17:27,454
Here is Marshall's line again, where we left
it.

167
00:17:27,899 --> 00:17:34,764
Marshall's line stays exactly where it is. It
is not wrong about arithmetic. But the line

168
00:17:34,764 --> 00:17:38,774
where Nell should actually build slides up
above it.

169
00:17:40,599 --> 00:17:46,933
And that gap has a meaning. It is the value of
the chance to wait — the thing she destroys

170
00:17:46,933 --> 00:17:48,482
the moment she builds.

171
00:17:50,299 --> 00:17:57,218
Build now, and the chance to wait is gone for
ever. She has to be paid for that, and the gap

172
00:17:57,218 --> 00:17:58,196
is the price.

173
00:18:00,033 --> 00:18:06,624
There is a mirror image of this on the way
down, and chapter fifteen builds it properly.

174
00:18:07,066 --> 00:18:12,888
Marshall says: shut down when the money stops
covering the day-to-day costs.

175
00:18:13,333 --> 00:18:19,630
The right answer is lower. She should absorb
real losses before she gives up — because

176
00:18:19,630 --> 00:18:23,877
giving up throws away the chance of things
getting better.

177
00:18:26,699 --> 00:18:29,455
waiting costs the profit given up

178
00:18:29,599 --> 00:18:32,821
so there is a trigger: high enough to act

179
00:18:32,966 --> 00:18:36,072
the trigger sits above the textbook line

180
00:18:36,199 --> 00:18:39,270
and the mirror image on the way down

181
00:18:48,266 --> 00:18:55,467
Forget factories for two minutes. Somebody
gives Nell a voucher. It says: you may buy this

182
00:18:55,467 --> 00:19:02,749
bicycle for forty pounds, on any day you like.
It never expires. And it never forces you to

183
00:19:02,749 --> 00:19:03,789
buy anything.

184
00:19:05,633 --> 00:19:09,420
Question. Is the voucher worth anything before
she uses it?

185
00:19:11,266 --> 00:19:18,445
Of course it is. Somebody would buy it off
her. It gives her a right she would not otherwise

186
00:19:18,445 --> 00:19:22,580
have, and it never obliges her to do anything
at all.

187
00:19:24,399 --> 00:19:31,088
And here is the point. The moment she uses it,
the voucher is gone. She has a bicycle

188
00:19:31,088 --> 00:19:35,809
instead. She cannot use it again, and she
cannot sell it on.

189
00:19:39,333 --> 00:19:42,206
The voucher is the chance to build.

190
00:19:42,633 --> 00:19:47,654
The forty pounds is the sunk cost — the money
she cannot get back.

191
00:19:48,099 --> 00:19:52,668
And the bicycle is all the revenue the factory
will ever earn.

192
00:19:57,899 --> 00:20:02,741
intrinsic value. What using the chance right
now would give you.

193
00:20:07,533 --> 00:20:11,602
holding premium. What the waiting itself is
worth.

194
00:20:15,933 --> 00:20:23,110
Which gives the rule, in one line. Use the
chance when the holding premium has fallen to

195
00:20:23,110 --> 00:20:24,497
zero. Not before.

196
00:20:26,333 --> 00:20:32,794
Before that point, waiting is still worth
something, and building throws it away for

197
00:20:32,794 --> 00:20:33,409
nothing.

198
00:20:36,233 --> 00:20:39,048
a right, not an obligation

199
00:20:39,166 --> 00:20:41,842
worth something before it is used

200
00:20:41,966 --> 00:20:44,979
intrinsic value: using it now

201
00:20:45,099 --> 00:20:49,114
holding premium → zero, then build

202
00:20:57,499 --> 00:21:02,851
So far this is a story. Is the effect big
enough to matter, or is it a curiosity?

203
00:21:03,299 --> 00:21:10,637
The paper answers that with arithmetic. There
is mathematics behind it, and for now we leave

204
00:21:10,637 --> 00:21:15,262
it sealed in a box. Two dials go in. One
number comes out.

205
00:21:15,699 --> 00:21:22,028
Dial one. How choppy the revenue is — how much
the money coming in swings around in a

206
00:21:22,028 --> 00:21:22,996
typical year.

207
00:21:23,433 --> 00:21:30,359
The paper gives anchors. About ten per cent a
year for revenue that moves with exchange

208
00:21:30,359 --> 00:21:37,683
rates. Twenty-five to forty for an oil well or
a copper mine. The base case sits between, at

209
00:21:37,683 --> 00:21:38,240
twenty.

210
00:21:40,566 --> 00:21:46,925
Dial two is the cost of capital, from chapter
two. What the money has to earn to be worth

211
00:21:46,925 --> 00:21:47,354
using.

212
00:21:47,799 --> 00:21:54,449
And out of the box comes one number. A
multiplier. The money coming in must reach that

213
00:21:54,449 --> 00:21:59,474
multiplier, times the break-even level, before
building is right.

214
00:22:05,799 --> 00:22:13,390
Base case. Cost of capital five per cent,
choppiness twenty per cent. The multiplier is one

215
00:22:13,390 --> 00:22:14,725
point eight six.

216
00:22:16,566 --> 00:22:22,307
The money coming in must nearly double the
break-even level before she should build.

217
00:22:22,733 --> 00:22:29,791
Said the other way round, that is a hurdle
rate of nine point three per cent, against a true

218
00:22:29,791 --> 00:22:31,632
cost of capital of five.

219
00:22:33,966 --> 00:22:41,513
Now turn the choppiness dial up. Forty per
cent a year — an oil well, a copper mine, a trade

220
00:22:41,513 --> 00:22:46,435
where prices swing hard. Watch the bar while
the dial turns.

221
00:22:46,866 --> 00:22:53,737
The multiplier jumps to three point three two.
The hurdle rate to sixteen point six per

222
00:22:53,737 --> 00:22:54,131
cent.

223
00:22:55,966 --> 00:22:59,860
Turn it back, and the bar comes back down with
it.

224
00:23:02,899 --> 00:23:10,232
Now turn the other dial. Not up — down. Make
money cheaper. Cost of capital two per cent

225
00:23:10,232 --> 00:23:11,565
instead of five.

226
00:23:11,999 --> 00:23:18,856
And the multiplier goes up. Two point six one.
Cheaper money makes her more reluctant to

227
00:23:18,856 --> 00:23:20,102
build, not less.

228
00:23:22,333 --> 00:23:29,105
Say that again, because it is the strangest
result in the paper. Cheaper money makes waiting

229
00:23:29,105 --> 00:23:30,282
more attractive.

230
00:23:32,099 --> 00:23:38,848
Here is why. When money is cheap, the future
matters more to you. And if the future matters

231
00:23:38,848 --> 00:23:45,597
more, then finding things out about it is
worth more. So waiting to find out is worth more.

232
00:23:47,933 --> 00:23:54,031
If the world is very certain, or the future is
barely valued at all, the multiplier

233
00:23:54,031 --> 00:23:57,558
collapses to one — and Marshall is simply
right.

234
00:23:57,999 --> 00:24:04,823
And as uncertainty grows, or money becomes
nearly free, it grows without limit. In the

235
00:24:04,823 --> 00:24:10,219
paper's own words, the textbook analysis
becomes totally misleading.

236
00:24:12,466 --> 00:24:19,324
One footnote. If the revenue is also growing —
say two per cent a year — two things pull

237
00:24:19,324 --> 00:24:26,417
against each other, and the waiting effect
wins. The hurdle rate goes to thirteen point six

238
00:24:26,417 --> 00:24:31,326
per cent, and the trigger to three point nine
times break-even.

239
00:24:34,166 --> 00:24:41,540
One last way of seeing all of that at once.
The multiplier depends on two things and two

240
00:24:41,540 --> 00:24:46,567
things only. Two things in, one number out.
That is a shape.

241
00:24:48,299 --> 00:24:54,789
Along one way, the revenue gets choppier.
Along the other, the money gets cheaper. And

242
00:24:54,789 --> 00:24:56,600
upwards is a higher bar.

243
00:24:57,033 --> 00:24:58,919
And here is the shape.

244
00:25:03,033 --> 00:25:10,143
Look at the near corner. Calm revenue,
expensive money. The sheet is almost flat, and the

245
00:25:10,143 --> 00:25:16,055
multiplier is almost one. That is Marshall's
world, and in it he is right.

246
00:25:17,899 --> 00:25:25,052
Now follow it away from that corner. Every
step towards choppier revenue, and every step

247
00:25:25,052 --> 00:25:31,555
towards cheaper money, lifts the sheet. The
two effects do not cancel. They add.

248
00:25:33,399 --> 00:25:39,731
The three numbers you just watched are three
points on this one sheet. The base case.

249
00:25:40,166 --> 00:25:42,691
Choppier revenue — further along.

250
00:25:43,133 --> 00:25:46,738
And cheaper money — further along the other
way.

251
00:25:48,566 --> 00:25:55,751
Hold that picture. In chapter forty-one we
come back to this exact sheet, and watch a firm

252
00:25:55,751 --> 00:25:59,104
walked across it — in the wrong direction.

253
00:26:01,933 --> 00:26:08,340
And that resolves the first of our three
puzzles. Firms demanding fifteen per cent when

254
00:26:08,340 --> 00:26:14,232
their money costs them five are not being
short-sighted. They are being correct.

255
00:26:17,066 --> 00:26:20,102
two dials → one multiplier

256
00:26:20,233 --> 00:26:27,472
base case 1.86× · 9.3% against a true 5%

257
00:26:27,599 --> 00:26:34,153
choppier → 3.32 · cheaper money → 2.61

258
00:26:34,299 --> 00:26:38,252
15% is arithmetic, not stupidity

259
00:26:45,199 --> 00:26:50,738
The next five chapters do the mathematics.
Properly, and from the beginning. Nothing beyond

260
00:26:50,738 --> 00:26:56,157
square roots, fractions and percentages is
used, and everything is built in front of you.

261
00:26:58,065 --> 00:27:04,067
A hundred pounds today, and a hundred pounds
in a year. Which would you rather have?

262
00:27:04,499 --> 00:27:10,428
Today, obviously. Because you could put
today's hundred to work, and have more than a

263
00:27:10,428 --> 00:27:11,893
hundred by next year.

264
00:27:13,732 --> 00:27:19,974
Put a hundred pounds somewhere paying five per
cent, and in a year you have a hundred and

265
00:27:19,974 --> 00:27:20,325
five.

266
00:27:20,765 --> 00:27:27,431
Run that backwards, and it is a division.
Divide by one point nought five, and you have

267
00:27:27,431 --> 00:27:30,036
turned money later into money now.

268
00:27:33,565 --> 00:27:38,239
interest. The extra a borrower pays for the
use of money.

269
00:27:42,432 --> 00:27:47,803
discounting. Turning money you get later into
what it is worth today.

270
00:27:53,699 --> 00:28:01,019
A factory that earns five pounds a year, for
ever. Here is the five pounds, arriving year

271
00:28:01,019 --> 00:28:01,924
after year.

272
00:28:02,365 --> 00:28:09,596
But each one has to be discounted back. Year
one is divided once. Year two, twice. Year

273
00:28:09,596 --> 00:28:16,993
twenty, twenty times. So the far-off ones
shrink away almost to nothing — and that is why

274
00:28:16,993 --> 00:28:20,234
the total does not run off to infinity.

275
00:28:22,065 --> 00:28:29,060
Add up every one of them, all the way out to
for ever, and the sum is astonishingly simple.

276
00:28:29,060 --> 00:28:34,209
Five pounds, divided by nought point nought
five. A hundred pounds.

277
00:28:36,032 --> 00:28:43,023
That is the only piece of machinery you need.
The value of a stream that goes on for ever is

278
00:28:43,023 --> 00:28:49,710
the yearly amount divided by the rate. And the
rate we divide by is the cost of capital.

279
00:28:53,232 --> 00:28:57,719
Five pounds a year at five per cent is worth a
hundred.

280
00:28:58,565 --> 00:29:05,460
The same five pounds, when the rate is only
two per cent, is worth two hundred and fifty.

281
00:29:05,460 --> 00:29:08,947
Cheaper money makes future income worth more.

282
00:29:09,765 --> 00:29:17,140
And at ten per cent it is worth only fifty.
Expensive money makes future income worth less.

283
00:29:17,965 --> 00:29:24,043
Hold on to the middle one. It is going to
matter enormously in chapter thirteen.

284
00:29:26,865 --> 00:29:30,190
money later is worth less than money now

285
00:29:30,332 --> 00:29:34,088
discounting turns later money into today's

286
00:29:34,232 --> 00:29:38,731
a stream for ever = amount ÷ the rate

287
00:29:38,865 --> 00:29:42,308
cheaper money makes future income worth MORE

288
00:29:50,699 --> 00:29:57,799
Nell is deciding whether to build. There are
exactly two amounts, and we can write both of

289
00:29:57,799 --> 00:29:58,588
them down.

290
00:29:59,032 --> 00:30:05,482
What she gets: the money coming in each month,
for ever, discounted back. From the last

291
00:30:05,482 --> 00:30:12,080
chapter, that is the yearly amount divided by
the rate. Call the yearly amount R, and the

292
00:30:12,080 --> 00:30:13,933
rate the cost of capital.

293
00:30:15,765 --> 00:30:20,611
What she pays: the sunk cost of building it.
Call that K.

294
00:30:22,432 --> 00:30:29,564
Three letters, and nothing more. R is the
revenue a year. K is the sunk cost. And the third

295
00:30:29,564 --> 00:30:33,247
one we will only ever call the cost of
capital.

296
00:30:35,465 --> 00:30:40,266
Subtract one from the other and you have what
the project is worth.

297
00:30:42,765 --> 00:30:46,584
net worth. What you get, minus what you pay.

298
00:30:52,099 --> 00:30:58,223
The borderline is where the two are exactly
equal — where net worth is nothing at all.

299
00:30:58,665 --> 00:31:04,148
Add K to both sides. What she gets equals what
she pays.

300
00:31:04,565 --> 00:31:09,786
Multiply both sides by the rate. And there is
the textbook's line.

301
00:31:11,632 --> 00:31:18,020
That is Marshall's line, and now you know
exactly where it comes from. It is the interest on

302
00:31:18,020 --> 00:31:23,992
the money she sank into the building — the
normal return on capital, and nothing more.

303
00:31:27,532 --> 00:31:35,177
Dixit's worked example uses a sunk cost of
two, and a cost of capital of five per cent.

304
00:31:35,999 --> 00:31:42,612
So the normal return on that capital is nought
point nought five times two. A tenth.

305
00:31:43,432 --> 00:31:47,774
And the factory also costs one to run each
year.

306
00:31:48,599 --> 00:31:54,888
Add them: one, plus a tenth. One point one.
That is the number you have been staring at

307
00:31:54,888 --> 00:31:59,226
since chapter two, and you have just worked it
out yourself.

308
00:32:03,365 --> 00:32:07,632
what she gets: R ÷ ρ

309
00:32:07,765 --> 00:32:09,710
what she pays: K

310
00:32:09,832 --> 00:32:13,402
the borderline is where net worth is zero

311
00:32:13,532 --> 00:32:20,348
so the textbook line is M = C + ρK = 1.10

312
00:32:28,732 --> 00:32:36,126
Every number in the next two chapters rests on
one assumption about how revenue moves. Each

313
00:32:36,126 --> 00:32:43,195
year, revenue does one of two things. It goes
up by a fixed percentage, or down by one.

314
00:32:45,032 --> 00:32:51,949
Percentages rather than pounds, because a big
firm swings by big amounts and a small one by

315
00:32:51,949 --> 00:32:56,129
small amounts. That is the only realistic way
to do it.

316
00:32:59,665 --> 00:33:06,900
Do that again the next year, and the next.
Every point splits in two, and the possibilities

317
00:33:06,900 --> 00:33:07,536
fan out.

318
00:33:09,365 --> 00:33:15,629
Watch one possible path through it. That is
one future — one thing that might happen.

319
00:33:16,065 --> 00:33:23,767
And here are a dozen more. Nobody knows which
one happens. All anybody knows is the shape of

320
00:33:23,767 --> 00:33:24,437
the fan.

321
00:33:27,965 --> 00:33:33,477
a random walk. Each step is up or down, and
you cannot tell which.

322
00:33:39,165 --> 00:33:46,705
Take a typical year and ask how far revenue
moved. In a calm trade, not far. The outcomes

323
00:33:46,705 --> 00:33:47,468
bunch up.

324
00:33:47,899 --> 00:33:52,867
In a rough trade — oil, metals — they spread
right out.

325
00:33:54,699 --> 00:34:01,381
The width of that spread is the number the
paper calls the choppiness. It is the size of a

326
00:34:01,381 --> 00:34:07,841
typical yearly swing, as a percentage. Ten per
cent with exchange rates. Twenty-five to

327
00:34:07,841 --> 00:34:11,627
forty for an oil well. And the base case at
twenty.

328
00:34:13,465 --> 00:34:20,653
The paper writes it as a Greek letter. This
film never will. It is the choppiness, and it is

329
00:34:20,653 --> 00:34:21,669
a percentage.

330
00:34:24,499 --> 00:34:28,487
each year: up or down by a fixed percentage

331
00:34:28,632 --> 00:34:32,074
run it forward and the futures fan out

332
00:34:32,199 --> 00:34:35,536
nobody knows the path, only the fan

333
00:34:35,665 --> 00:34:38,458
the width of the fan is the choppiness

334
00:34:46,865 --> 00:34:53,036
The paper has two diagrams, and they are the
heart of it. Across the bottom, the revenue

335
00:34:53,036 --> 00:34:58,506
coming in each year. Up the side, what the
whole opportunity is worth to Nell.

336
00:34:58,931 --> 00:35:06,081
First, the straight line. If she builds today
she gets the revenue for ever, divided by the

337
00:35:06,081 --> 00:35:13,231
rate, minus the sunk cost. That is the sum
from two chapters ago, and drawn against revenue

338
00:35:13,231 --> 00:35:14,959
it is a straight line.

339
00:35:16,798 --> 00:35:24,619
It starts below the axis, because at zero
revenue she would simply lose the sunk cost. And

340
00:35:24,619 --> 00:35:28,356
it crosses zero exactly at Marshall's line.

341
00:35:30,198 --> 00:35:37,065
Now the second line, and this one is a curve.
It is what the chance to build is worth if she

342
00:35:37,065 --> 00:35:38,557
does not use it yet.

343
00:35:40,398 --> 00:35:46,594
When revenue is very low the chance is nearly
worthless — things would have to improve

344
00:35:46,594 --> 00:35:49,404
enormously, and that is a long way off.

345
00:35:49,831 --> 00:35:56,825
As revenue rises the chance is worth more, and
worth more faster. That is why it bends

346
00:35:56,825 --> 00:35:57,476
upwards.

347
00:35:57,898 --> 00:36:04,542
And close to the point where she would build,
it is worth just about what building is worth.

348
00:36:04,542 --> 00:36:06,058
The two have to meet.

349
00:36:08,398 --> 00:36:14,972
They meet here. And they do not cross — they
touch, and run off in the same direction.

350
00:36:16,798 --> 00:36:23,395
If the two lines crossed, waiting would still
be worth more just past the meeting point — so

351
00:36:23,395 --> 00:36:25,976
she would not build there after all.

352
00:36:26,398 --> 00:36:32,472
And if the curve stayed below the line, she
would have been better off building sooner.

353
00:36:32,898 --> 00:36:39,789
Touching, and only touching, is the right
place. That single condition is what picks out the

354
00:36:39,789 --> 00:36:40,388
trigger.

355
00:36:42,731 --> 00:36:49,020
And there, between where the textbook says
build and where she actually should, is the gap

356
00:36:49,020 --> 00:36:54,051
this whole film is about. You have now seen
exactly where it comes from.

357
00:36:55,898 --> 00:37:02,585
The paper finds that touching point with
calculus. We are about to get exactly the same

358
00:37:02,585 --> 00:37:04,584
answer with a square root.

359
00:37:07,431 --> 00:37:09,936
the straight line: building now

360
00:37:10,065 --> 00:37:12,776
the curve: what waiting is worth

361
00:37:12,898 --> 00:37:15,516
they touch — they do not cross

362
00:37:15,665 --> 00:37:18,236
and where they touch is the trigger

363
00:37:26,631 --> 00:37:33,292
Before any of this, the alphabet again, with
two new ones added. Economists write these

364
00:37:33,292 --> 00:37:40,335
quantities as Greek letters purely to save
space. Each one is a short name for something you

365
00:37:40,335 --> 00:37:41,330
already know.

366
00:37:41,765 --> 00:37:44,868
R is the money coming in, as before.

367
00:37:45,298 --> 00:37:48,961
K is the sunk cost of building, as before.

368
00:37:49,398 --> 00:37:55,023
The one that looks like a p is the cost of
capital — you met it in chapter ten.

369
00:37:55,465 --> 00:38:00,718
The one like an o with a tail is the
choppiness, from chapter eleven.

370
00:38:01,165 --> 00:38:08,049
And the one like a B is new. It is the
steepness, and working it out is what this chapter

371
00:38:08,049 --> 00:38:08,436
does.

372
00:38:08,865 --> 00:38:15,410
Nothing in this film is ever said as a Greek
letter. Every one of them is spoken as the

373
00:38:15,410 --> 00:38:18,720
English phrase beside it, every single time.

374
00:38:22,265 --> 00:38:29,874
Start inside. Eight, times the cost of
capital, divided by the choppiness multiplied by

375
00:38:29,874 --> 00:38:30,487
itself.

376
00:38:32,331 --> 00:38:39,517
Add one to it. Take the square root. Add one
again, and halve it. That is the whole formula.

377
00:38:41,365 --> 00:38:47,894
The paper calls the answer beta. This film
calls it the steepness, because that is what it

378
00:38:47,894 --> 00:38:52,102
measures — how steeply the value of waiting
curves upward.

379
00:38:55,631 --> 00:39:01,547
The choppiness is nought point two. Times
itself, nought point nought four.

380
00:39:02,298 --> 00:39:08,860
Eight times nought point nought five is nought
point four. Divided by nought point nought

381
00:39:08,860 --> 00:39:10,187
four, that is ten.

382
00:39:10,965 --> 00:39:16,975
One plus ten is eleven. The square root of
eleven is three point three one seven.

383
00:39:17,731 --> 00:39:24,599
One plus that is four point three one seven.
Halve it. Two point one five eight.

384
00:39:25,365 --> 00:39:31,699
The paper prints two point one five. That is
your answer, with the paper's own rounding.

385
00:39:35,231 --> 00:39:38,767
Divide the steepness by itself-minus-one.

386
00:39:39,198 --> 00:39:46,693
Two point one five eight, divided by one point
one five eight. One point eight six. There is

387
00:39:46,693 --> 00:39:51,988
the multiplier from chapter eight — and you
have just derived it.

388
00:39:53,831 --> 00:39:58,757
Multiply the textbook's line by it, and you
have the real trigger.

389
00:39:59,198 --> 00:40:06,199
Or multiply the cost of capital by it, and you
have the corrected hurdle rate. Nine point

390
00:40:06,199 --> 00:40:07,379
three per cent.

391
00:40:10,898 --> 00:40:17,952
Turn the choppiness up to forty per cent.
Nought point four times itself is nought point one

392
00:40:17,952 --> 00:40:24,623
six. Eight times nought point nought five over
that is two and a half. One plus that is

393
00:40:24,623 --> 00:40:31,524
three and a half; its square root is one point
eight seven. Halve one plus that: one point

394
00:40:31,524 --> 00:40:32,751
four three five.

395
00:40:33,498 --> 00:40:40,163
Divide by itself-minus-one: three point three.
The paper prints three point three two,

396
00:40:40,163 --> 00:40:43,107
because it rounds the steepness first.

397
00:40:43,831 --> 00:40:50,789
Now put the choppiness back to twenty and drop
the cost of capital to two per cent. That

398
00:40:50,789 --> 00:40:57,746
gives four inside; one plus four is five; the
square root of five is two point two three

399
00:40:57,746 --> 00:41:01,699
six. Halve one plus that: one point six one
eight.

400
00:41:02,431 --> 00:41:08,161
And divide: two point six two. The paper
prints two point six one.

401
00:41:08,898 --> 00:41:15,058
And now you can see why both dials push the
same way. More choppiness, or cheaper money,

402
00:41:15,058 --> 00:41:21,499
both make the number inside the square root
smaller. That makes the steepness smaller. And a

403
00:41:21,499 --> 00:41:24,579
smaller steepness gives a bigger multiplier.

404
00:41:28,098 --> 00:41:35,082
Let the choppiness shrink to nothing. The
number inside the square root grows without limit,

405
00:41:35,082 --> 00:41:41,686
the steepness grows with it, and the
multiplier settles on one. The textbook is exactly

406
00:41:41,686 --> 00:41:43,583
right in a certain world.

407
00:41:44,431 --> 00:41:50,719
Let the cost of capital fall to nothing
instead. The steepness goes to one, and the

408
00:41:50,719 --> 00:41:57,158
multiplier goes off to infinity. Which is the
paper's own line: the textbook analysis

409
00:41:57,158 --> 00:41:59,203
becomes totally misleading.

410
00:42:00,031 --> 00:42:06,397
One honest note to finish on. All of that
works because the project, once built, always

411
00:42:06,397 --> 00:42:13,054
makes money. Once you let it make losses and
allow her to give up, the two lines have to be

412
00:42:13,054 --> 00:42:19,712
found together, and there is no formula — it
needs four equations and a computer. The paper

413
00:42:19,712 --> 00:42:25,638
does that, and prints the answers: nought
point seven two, and one point six two.

414
00:42:28,465 --> 00:42:33,474
one square root gives the steepness: 2.158

415
00:42:33,598 --> 00:42:39,664
steepness ÷ (steepness − 1) = 1.86

416
00:42:39,798 --> 00:42:47,942
40% choppiness → 3.32 · 2% money → 2.61

417
00:42:48,065 --> 00:42:52,099
both dials shrink it, so both raise the bar

418
00:43:04,998 --> 00:43:11,738
Start with a straight line, which you met at
school. Its steepness is one number, and it is

419
00:43:11,738 --> 00:43:13,886
the same everywhere along it.

420
00:43:14,331 --> 00:43:21,429
Go along, and go up. Steepness is the up
divided by the along. Nothing more than that.

421
00:43:25,765 --> 00:43:33,327
Now bend it. A curve is not equally steep
everywhere. Down here it is gentle. Up there it is

422
00:43:33,327 --> 00:43:33,820
steep.

423
00:43:38,665 --> 00:43:45,737
Take the point you care about, and a second
point further along. Join them. That line has a

424
00:43:45,737 --> 00:43:50,555
steepness you can work out — up over along,
exactly as before.

425
00:43:52,398 --> 00:43:58,778
Now slide the second point towards the first,
and watch what that line does.

426
00:44:00,598 --> 00:44:07,463
It settles on one line: the line that just
touches the curve at that point, and does not

427
00:44:07,463 --> 00:44:11,519
cross it. That is the steepness of the curve,
there.

428
00:44:15,065 --> 00:44:20,144
the gradient. How steep a curve is at one
single point on it.

429
00:44:25,165 --> 00:44:31,301
Write the value of something as V of R — the
value, when the money coming in is R.

430
00:44:31,731 --> 00:44:38,737
Then V, with a dash, means its gradient: how
fast that value changes as the money coming in

431
00:44:38,737 --> 00:44:44,665
changes. A dash is the whole of the notation.
There is nothing else to learn.

432
00:44:46,498 --> 00:44:53,541
And one fact you will need, which you can take
on trust or check on paper. R squared has

433
00:44:53,541 --> 00:44:57,783
gradient two R. R cubed has gradient three R
squared.

434
00:44:58,231 --> 00:45:05,543
In general: bring the power down in front, and
knock one off it. That single rule is all the

435
00:45:05,543 --> 00:45:07,530
calculus this film needs.

436
00:45:10,365 --> 00:45:13,482
steepness of a line: up over along

437
00:45:13,631 --> 00:45:17,062
a curve is a different steepness everywhere

438
00:45:17,198 --> 00:45:21,024
slide two points together → the tangent

439
00:45:21,165 --> 00:45:26,024
and the rule: R^x has gradient x R^(x−1)

440
00:45:36,631 --> 00:45:43,728
Here is the tangent again, and here it is
walking along the curve. Watch the tangent itself,

441
00:45:43,728 --> 00:45:44,808
not the point.

442
00:45:45,231 --> 00:45:51,626
It is turning. The gradient is not a fixed
number: it has a gradient of its own.

443
00:45:55,165 --> 00:46:00,778
the second gradient. How fast the steepness
itself is changing.

444
00:46:04,298 --> 00:46:11,740
One dash for the steepness. Two dashes for how
fast the steepness is changing. That is all

445
00:46:11,740 --> 00:46:13,477
the second one means.

446
00:46:19,198 --> 00:46:26,585
Two futures, equally likely. One bad, one
good, the same distance either side of where we

447
00:46:26,585 --> 00:46:27,249
are now.

448
00:46:27,698 --> 00:46:33,539
Join them. The middle of that line is the
average of the two values — what you get on

449
00:46:33,539 --> 00:46:36,838
average if the future goes one way or the
other.

450
00:46:37,265 --> 00:46:43,644
And it is higher than the curve. The average
of the two values beats the value at the

451
00:46:43,644 --> 00:46:49,723
average. The gap is pure bend — and it is
exactly the gap chapter five was about.

452
00:46:51,565 --> 00:46:58,125
So a value that bends upwards is worth more
when the future is uncertain than when it is

453
00:46:58,125 --> 00:47:02,970
not. That is the whole reason the second
gradient appears at all.

454
00:47:08,398 --> 00:47:15,225
A curve that bends the other way loses by the
same argument. The chord sits below it, so

455
00:47:15,225 --> 00:47:17,940
uncertainty costs rather than pays.

456
00:47:18,365 --> 00:47:21,274
Bending up, uncertainty pays.

457
00:47:21,698 --> 00:47:24,214
Bending down, it costs.

458
00:47:24,631 --> 00:47:31,712
And a straight line — no bend at all — is the
one case where uncertainty makes no difference

459
00:47:31,712 --> 00:47:38,715
whatsoever. Which is precisely why the
textbook, which draws straight lines, never sees any

460
00:47:38,715 --> 00:47:39,330
of this.

461
00:47:42,165 --> 00:47:46,211
two dashes: how fast the steepness changes

462
00:47:46,331 --> 00:47:49,693
a bend up puts the average above the curve

463
00:47:49,831 --> 00:47:53,355
so uncertainty adds value to a bent thing

464
00:47:53,498 --> 00:47:56,836
and does nothing at all to a straight one

465
00:48:09,731 --> 00:48:17,012
Here is the money coming in, wandering about
as it does. Cut out one tiny slice of time from

466
00:48:17,012 --> 00:48:17,249
it.

467
00:48:17,698 --> 00:48:25,000
Call the length of that slice d t. The d is
just shorthand for a change in something, so d t

468
00:48:25,000 --> 00:48:28,095
is a change in time — a very small one.

469
00:48:29,931 --> 00:48:37,399
And d R is the change in the money coming in
over that same slice. Nobody knows which way it

470
00:48:37,399 --> 00:48:38,048
will go.

471
00:48:41,565 --> 00:48:48,492
The paper writes down two facts about it. E
square brackets means on average. On average,

472
00:48:48,492 --> 00:48:55,576
the money drifts by a rate called the drift,
times where it is now, times the length of the

473
00:48:55,576 --> 00:48:56,043
slice.

474
00:48:57,865 --> 00:49:04,737
And around that average it scatters. The
scatter is measured by the choppiness from chapter

475
00:49:04,737 --> 00:49:09,646
eleven, squared, times where it is now,
squared, times the slice.

476
00:49:13,165 --> 00:49:19,605
Here is the thing that makes all of this work.
Take a step up and a step down, equally

477
00:49:19,605 --> 00:49:23,574
likely. Add them and average. They cancel to
nothing.

478
00:49:25,398 --> 00:49:32,198
Now square them first. Minus two squared is
four, the same as plus two squared. Squaring

479
00:49:32,198 --> 00:49:38,381
throws the minus sign away, so the average of
the squares is not nothing at all.

480
00:49:40,198 --> 00:49:46,840
Randomness disappears when you average it, and
survives when you square it first. That is

481
00:49:46,840 --> 00:49:53,557
the whole reason a second gradient — which
multiplies a square — has anything to say here.

482
00:49:57,098 --> 00:50:03,532
The average of the square is the square of the
average, plus the scatter. That is a standard

483
00:50:03,532 --> 00:50:07,308
fact about averages, and it is the only one we
borrow.

484
00:50:07,731 --> 00:50:13,607
Put the two facts in, and you get a d t
squared term and a d t term.

485
00:50:15,431 --> 00:50:22,553
And now the step that makes the whole thing
tractable. If d t is a hundredth, d t squared is

486
00:50:22,553 --> 00:50:29,441
a ten-thousandth. Make the slice small enough
and the squared term is nothing next to the

487
00:50:29,441 --> 00:50:31,144
other one. So drop it.

488
00:50:33,965 --> 00:50:38,570
dt is a tiny slice of time; dR the change in
it

489
00:50:38,698 --> 00:50:42,814
on average it drifts; around that it scatters

490
00:50:42,931 --> 00:50:46,641
a square keeps what an average throws away

491
00:50:46,765 --> 00:50:51,055
so the squared step survives, and nothing else
does

492
00:51:03,964 --> 00:51:10,186
Take the value curve and stand at one point on
it. Close up, the tangent is a good

493
00:51:10,186 --> 00:51:12,083
description of the curve.

494
00:51:12,531 --> 00:51:19,390
But it is not perfect. Move a little way, and
the real curve has lifted off the tangent — by

495
00:51:19,390 --> 00:51:21,999
an amount that depends on the bend.

496
00:51:25,531 --> 00:51:32,725
Which gives the line the appendix opens with.
The expected change in value has two pieces.

497
00:51:33,164 --> 00:51:38,606
The first is the tangent's answer: the slope,
times how far you moved.

498
00:51:39,031 --> 00:51:45,393
The second is the correction for the bend: a
half of the second gradient, times the square

499
00:51:45,393 --> 00:51:51,896
of the step. And we know what that square is —
it is the one thing randomness leaves behind.

500
00:51:53,731 --> 00:52:00,558
Put the two facts from the last chapter in,
and every piece of it is now something we have

501
00:52:00,558 --> 00:52:01,620
already built.

502
00:52:05,164 --> 00:52:11,787
The chance to build is an asset you are
holding. It pays you nothing while you hold it — no

503
00:52:11,787 --> 00:52:16,736
rent, no revenue — so all it can offer is the
gain in its own value.

504
00:52:17,164 --> 00:52:23,492
And your money could have been earning the
ordinary return somewhere else. In equilibrium

505
00:52:23,492 --> 00:52:26,762
those two must match, or nobody would hold it.

506
00:52:28,597 --> 00:52:33,076
Expected gain equals the normal return. One
line.

507
00:52:38,597 --> 00:52:47,287
Every single term has a d t in it. So divide
the whole line by d t, and every d t disappears

508
00:52:47,287 --> 00:52:48,042
at once.

509
00:52:48,464 --> 00:52:54,979
Tidy it up, bring everything to one side, and
this is equation A one of the appendix. It is

510
00:52:54,979 --> 00:53:01,566
the whole of the paper's mathematics in a
single line, and you have just watched every piece

511
00:53:01,566 --> 00:53:02,854
of it being built.

512
00:53:04,897 --> 00:53:09,842
Read it left to right. A bend term, carrying
the choppiness.

513
00:53:10,264 --> 00:53:13,180
A slope term, carrying the drift.

514
00:53:13,597 --> 00:53:17,969
And the ordinary return being given up by
holding on.

515
00:53:20,797 --> 00:53:24,542
close up, a curve is a tangent plus a bend

516
00:53:24,664 --> 00:53:28,942
so E[dV] has a slope term and a bend term

517
00:53:29,064 --> 00:53:33,563
waiting pays nothing, so gain = normal return

518
00:53:33,697 --> 00:53:38,150
divide by the slice, and the equation is left

519
00:53:52,864 --> 00:53:59,506
Look at the shape of it. Wherever there are
two dashes there is an R squared, and wherever

520
00:53:59,506 --> 00:54:06,222
there is one dash there is a single R. Each
differentiation is paid for with exactly one R.

521
00:54:06,664 --> 00:54:14,480
So try a power. Using the rule from chapter
fourteen, the gradient of R to the x is x times

522
00:54:14,480 --> 00:54:16,284
R to the x minus one.

523
00:54:16,731 --> 00:54:23,673
Multiply that by the R sitting next to it, and
the power comes straight back to what it was.

524
00:54:23,673 --> 00:54:28,125
The shape survives. That is why a power is the
right guess.

525
00:54:31,664 --> 00:54:34,839
The value itself is R to the x.

526
00:54:35,264 --> 00:54:39,908
R times its gradient is x, times R to the x.

527
00:54:40,331 --> 00:54:48,117
And R squared times the second gradient is x,
times x minus one, times R to the x. Every one

528
00:54:48,117 --> 00:54:53,618
of them is the same R to the x, with a
different number in front.

529
00:54:55,464 --> 00:55:01,403
Put them into the equation and the whole R to
the x factors out of every term.

530
00:55:01,831 --> 00:55:09,090
R to the x is never zero, so the bracket must
be. And the bracket is a quadratic — the thing

531
00:55:09,090 --> 00:55:12,640
you solved at school. That is equation A two.

532
00:55:18,364 --> 00:55:25,467
A quadratic crosses zero twice. The paper
calls the two answers by the first two letters of

533
00:55:25,467 --> 00:55:31,243
the Greek alphabet. One of them is negative.
The other is bigger than one.

534
00:55:33,064 --> 00:55:38,770
So the general answer is a bit of each, with
two unknown amounts A and B.

535
00:55:39,197 --> 00:55:46,324
Now one piece of economics kills half of it.
If the money coming in falls to nothing, the

536
00:55:46,324 --> 00:55:53,691
chance to build is worth nothing. But R to a
negative power blows up as R goes to zero. So A

537
00:55:53,691 --> 00:55:54,892
has to be zero.

538
00:55:55,331 --> 00:56:02,045
What is left is the paper's equation two,
which chapter twelve drew as a curve without ever

539
00:56:02,045 --> 00:56:03,963
saying where it came from.

540
00:56:07,497 --> 00:56:11,574
Set the drift to zero, as the main text does.

541
00:56:11,997 --> 00:56:15,063
Divide through by a half sigma squared.

542
00:56:15,497 --> 00:56:17,825
Multiply out the bracket.

543
00:56:18,264 --> 00:56:24,983
Now complete the square — add a quarter to
both sides, which is exactly what you were taught

544
00:56:24,983 --> 00:56:25,422
to do.

545
00:56:25,864 --> 00:56:30,885
Tidy the right-hand side over a common
denominator of four.

546
00:56:31,331 --> 00:56:37,756
Square-root both sides, take the bigger
answer, and there is the formula from chapter

547
00:56:37,756 --> 00:56:42,292
thirteen. Every symbol in it has now been
derived on screen.

548
00:56:47,731 --> 00:56:51,371
each dash is paid for with exactly one R

549
00:56:51,497 --> 00:56:55,369
so a power survives, and R^x factors out

550
00:56:55,497 --> 00:56:58,847
leaving a quadratic, with two roots

551
00:56:58,964 --> 00:57:03,115
complete the square, and the steepness falls
out

552
00:57:16,030 --> 00:57:22,526
Everything so far was about the chance to
build, which pays you nothing while you hold it.

553
00:57:22,963 --> 00:57:29,695
A project that has actually been built is
different. It hands you the money coming in, every

554
00:57:29,695 --> 00:57:34,378
year, while you own it. So the balance has one
more thing on it.

555
00:57:34,797 --> 00:57:42,017
Which is the same equation with a single extra
R on the end — the revenue it pays out. That

556
00:57:42,017 --> 00:57:43,525
is equation A five.

557
00:57:43,963 --> 00:57:50,329
Its answer is the plain worth of the revenue
stream, plus a term that is the value of the

558
00:57:50,329 --> 00:57:56,767
option to give up. That option is worthless
when takings are enormous, which is what fixes

559
00:57:56,767 --> 00:57:58,984
which power survives this time.

560
00:58:03,430 --> 00:58:09,967
Here is what waiting is worth: B times R to
the beta, the curve we just derived.

561
00:58:10,397 --> 00:58:16,664
And here is what a running project is worth,
once you allow it to be abandoned. Nearly a

562
00:58:16,664 --> 00:58:21,152
straight line when takings are high, bending
away as they fall.

563
00:58:24,197 --> 00:58:30,884
At the level where she builds, stepping from
one curve to the other must be worth exactly

564
00:58:30,884 --> 00:58:37,645
the cost of building. Not more, or she would
have built sooner. Not less, or she would not

565
00:58:37,645 --> 00:58:38,622
build at all.

566
00:58:41,863 --> 00:58:48,271
And a second condition, which is the subtle
one. The two curves must not just be the right

567
00:58:48,271 --> 00:58:54,038
distance apart — they must have the same
steepness there. They meet tangentially.

568
00:58:54,463 --> 00:59:00,818
Because a corner would mean the trigger was in
the wrong place: nudge it either way and you

569
00:59:00,818 --> 00:59:06,056
would do better. Only where the slopes agree
is there nothing left to gain.

570
00:59:09,597 --> 00:59:15,454
smooth pasting. Where two value curves meet,
their slopes must agree.

571
00:59:20,497 --> 00:59:24,775
At the build line, the step up is worth the
cost.

572
00:59:25,197 --> 00:59:27,376
And the slopes agree there.

573
00:59:27,797 --> 00:59:33,654
At the give-up line, the step is worth nothing
at all — she simply walks away.

574
00:59:34,097 --> 00:59:36,680
And the slopes agree there too.

575
00:59:37,097 --> 00:59:42,800
Four conditions. Four unknowns: the two
amounts, and the two levels.

576
00:59:44,630 --> 00:59:51,457
And the paper is honest about what happens
next. There is no formula. The four have to be

577
00:59:51,457 --> 00:59:58,361
solved together on a computer. Which is
exactly why the two numbers chapter thirteen ended

578
00:59:58,361 --> 01:00:04,728
on were printed rather than derived: nought
point seven two, and one point six two.

579
01:00:07,563 --> 01:00:12,550
a running project pays R, so its equation
gains +R

580
01:00:12,697 --> 01:00:16,685
at H the step between curves is worth K

581
01:00:16,830 --> 01:00:20,342
and the slopes must agree: smooth pasting

582
01:00:20,463 --> 01:00:24,835
four conditions, four unknowns, solved by
computer

583
01:00:31,763 --> 01:00:38,717
Being honest about the limits is part of the
teaching, and it also stops you over-applying

584
01:00:38,717 --> 01:00:42,502
the idea. The paper sets out four
qualifications.

585
01:00:44,397 --> 01:00:52,104
Everything so far assumed the chance was
Nell's alone. Suppose it is not. Suppose there is

586
01:00:52,104 --> 01:00:56,558
one site, or one licence, and several firms
want it.

587
01:00:56,997 --> 01:01:01,667
Nell hesitates, sensibly, to see how things
develop.

588
01:01:02,097 --> 01:01:05,133
And somebody else walks through the door.

589
01:01:05,563 --> 01:01:12,515
When the chance can be snatched, waiting is
not possible. And when waiting is not possible,

590
01:01:12,515 --> 01:01:19,314
the textbook's trigger is valid again. This is
a real limit, and Part Three comes back to

591
01:01:19,314 --> 01:01:19,544
it.

592
01:01:23,063 --> 01:01:29,548
Sometimes moving first has a value of its own
— you take the best position, and everyone

593
01:01:29,548 --> 01:01:33,527
else has to work around you. That pulls the
other way.

594
01:01:33,963 --> 01:01:37,846
The right answer balances the two against each
other.

595
01:01:41,363 --> 01:01:48,572
Six firms, each looking at the same
opportunity, each with slightly different information

596
01:01:48,572 --> 01:01:49,301
about it.

597
01:01:49,730 --> 01:01:56,859
Each one looks around, sees that nobody else
has moved, and concludes the others must have

598
01:01:56,859 --> 01:02:02,324
found something discouraging. So each revises
its own view downwards.

599
01:02:02,763 --> 01:02:10,499
And so everybody waits. Not because the
opportunity is bad, but because everybody is reading

600
01:02:10,499 --> 01:02:12,601
everybody else's silence.

601
01:02:14,430 --> 01:02:16,603
Then one of them moves.

602
01:02:17,030 --> 01:02:23,828
And now everybody revises upwards — because
that firm must have seen something good.

603
01:02:23,828 --> 01:02:29,413
Investment arrives in a sudden bunch. Which is
why it comes in waves.

604
01:02:32,930 --> 01:02:40,809
This one comes with a quotation, because it is
the single idea Part Three leans on hardest.

605
01:02:40,809 --> 01:02:43,060
Dixit is quoting Bernanke.

606
01:02:44,897 --> 01:02:52,510
In plain words. When you can wait, it is
mainly the bad possible futures that decide whether

607
01:02:52,510 --> 01:02:53,668
you build now.

608
01:02:55,497 --> 01:03:01,873
Because the good futures will still be there
next year. You have not missed them. Waiting

609
01:03:01,873 --> 01:03:04,810
only ever protects you from the bad ones.

610
01:03:07,230 --> 01:03:13,399
Carry the qualification the paper adds in the
very next sentence, because dropping it

611
01:03:13,399 --> 01:03:19,714
changes the meaning. The total chance of
ending up above the trigger does still matter.

612
01:03:20,163 --> 01:03:26,527
What does not matter is the shape of the good
outcomes beyond it. Those are not the same

613
01:03:26,527 --> 01:03:29,636
thing, and Part Three needs the difference.

614
01:03:32,463 --> 01:03:37,009
a race → waiting is impossible, textbook right

615
01:03:37,130 --> 01:03:40,282
moving first can pull the other way

616
01:03:40,430 --> 01:03:44,674
inaction reads as bad news → bunching

617
01:03:44,797 --> 01:03:48,518
the bad news principle, with its qualification

618
01:03:58,630 --> 01:04:03,083
suspending. Stopping for a while, meaning to
start again.

619
01:04:07,297 --> 01:04:09,915
abandoning. Stopping for good.

620
01:04:12,263 --> 01:04:18,341
To make the point sharp, the paper assumes
that idle machinery rusts. Totally, and

621
01:04:18,341 --> 01:04:25,085
immediately. So pausing and quitting become
the same thing, and starting again means paying

622
01:04:25,085 --> 01:04:27,383
the whole sunk cost over again.

623
01:04:29,230 --> 01:04:36,008
And the paper flags that itself, as a
deliberate simplification. Where a firm really can

624
01:04:36,008 --> 01:04:43,093
mothball a factory and restart it cheaply,
this half of the argument gets weaker. Hold on to

625
01:04:43,093 --> 01:04:45,173
that — Part Three needs it.

626
01:04:49,397 --> 01:04:52,398
Nell is losing money this month.

627
01:04:52,830 --> 01:04:59,291
If she closes, she can never get the chance
back without paying for it all over again.

628
01:04:59,730 --> 01:05:04,403
Staying open keeps alive the chance that trade
recovers.

629
01:05:04,830 --> 01:05:11,895
And that chance is worth something. So she
should absorb real losses before she shuts.

630
01:05:17,030 --> 01:05:24,095
Down here, at nought point seven two, she
finally gives up. That is the real quit-line.

631
01:05:24,930 --> 01:05:32,018
At one, her day-to-day costs are exactly
covered. That is where the textbook says stop.

632
01:05:32,863 --> 01:05:39,600
At one point one, the textbook says build —
day-to-day costs plus the normal return on the

633
01:05:39,600 --> 01:05:40,648
money sunk in.

634
01:05:41,497 --> 01:05:46,054
And at one point six two, she should actually
build.

635
01:05:47,197 --> 01:05:54,156
At the true build point her operating profit
is nought point six two — more than six times

636
01:05:54,156 --> 01:05:57,791
the normal return on the money she has sunk
in.

637
01:05:58,230 --> 01:06:04,812
And at the quit point she is absorbing losses
of nearly a third of her day-to-day costs,

638
01:06:04,812 --> 01:06:07,729
month after month, before she gives up.

639
01:06:11,163 --> 01:06:17,855
Suppose the building costs more to put up. A
bigger sunk cost. What happens to the two

640
01:06:17,855 --> 01:06:18,322
lines?

641
01:06:18,763 --> 01:06:23,204
The build-line goes up. She is more reluctant
to start.

642
01:06:23,630 --> 01:06:28,145
And the quit-line goes down. She is more
reluctant to stop.

643
01:06:29,963 --> 01:06:36,992
Say that one twice, because it is not obvious.
A bigger stake to keep alive makes her slower

644
01:06:36,992 --> 01:06:43,485
to start and slower to stop. Both at once, and
for the same reason — there is more to

645
01:06:43,485 --> 01:06:44,096
protect.

646
01:06:46,430 --> 01:06:53,450
One more. If leaving is itself expensive —
redundancy payments, restoring the site of a mine

647
01:06:53,450 --> 01:07:00,470
— then the build-line rises further still. She
is more cautious about entering something she

648
01:07:00,470 --> 01:07:02,454
may have to pay to escape.

649
01:07:05,297 --> 01:07:09,494
stopping = ruin, a flagged simplification

650
01:07:09,630 --> 01:07:14,524
staying keeps recovery alive, so losses are
absorbed

651
01:07:14,663 --> 01:07:21,670
0.72 · 1.00 · 1.10 · 1.62

652
01:07:21,797 --> 01:07:25,390
a bigger sunk cost slows her both ways

653
01:07:34,796 --> 01:07:41,759
Take away the uncertainty for a moment, and
leave only the sunk cost. The textbook still has

654
01:07:41,759 --> 01:07:47,133
a small band where doing nothing is right —
from one, to one point one.

655
01:07:47,563 --> 01:07:51,911
That is the whole of it. A band nought point
one wide.

656
01:07:53,729 --> 01:07:56,765
Now put the uncertainty back in.

657
01:07:57,196 --> 01:08:03,390
Nought point seven two, to one point six two.
Nought point nine wide.

658
01:08:05,229 --> 01:08:12,298
About nine times wider. That comparison is
ours, not the paper's — the paper prints the four

659
01:08:12,298 --> 01:08:18,522
levels and calls the difference quite a
dramatic difference. We did the division.

660
01:08:20,363 --> 01:08:26,483
This band has a name. The zone of inaction.
The stretch where the right thing to do is

661
01:08:26,483 --> 01:08:27,551
nothing at all.

662
01:08:31,096 --> 01:08:37,658
First. Small frictions can produce much larger
rigidities than models which ignore

663
01:08:37,658 --> 01:08:45,020
gradually-arriving information would ever
suggest. A little stickiness goes a very long way.

664
01:08:47,263 --> 01:08:54,780
Second, and this one reaches outside the firm
altogether. Hiring and firing cost money. So

665
01:08:54,780 --> 01:08:58,038
exactly the same logic applies to jobs.

666
01:08:58,463 --> 01:09:01,864
Employers hoard labour in downturns.

667
01:09:02,296 --> 01:09:05,423
And they are slow to hire in upturns.

668
01:09:05,863 --> 01:09:12,099
So what a worker adds to the business can sit
well above the wage without anybody being

669
01:09:12,099 --> 01:09:16,042
hired — and well below it without anybody
being let go.

670
01:09:18,363 --> 01:09:25,080
And the paper draws a conclusion from that
which is worth hearing. The popular worry about

671
01:09:25,080 --> 01:09:31,797
loss of jobs — the one economists usually wave
away — may have more justification than the

672
01:09:31,797 --> 01:09:32,991
textbook allows.

673
01:09:35,829 --> 01:09:41,443
sunk costs alone: 1.00 – 1.10

674
01:09:41,563 --> 01:09:46,573
with uncertainty: 0.72 – 1.62

675
01:09:46,696 --> 01:09:50,626
small frictions → large rigidities

676
01:09:50,763 --> 01:09:53,868
and the same logic applies to jobs

677
01:10:04,963 --> 01:10:12,525
She starts at one. Money coming in exactly
covers the day-to-day costs. Nothing is built.

678
01:10:12,996 --> 01:10:20,050
It rises. It crosses one point one — the
textbook's build-line — and nothing happens. She

679
01:10:20,050 --> 01:10:21,239
does not build.

680
01:10:21,696 --> 01:10:27,008
It keeps rising. And at one point six two, she
builds.

681
01:10:28,263 --> 01:10:35,674
Now the money coming in starts to fall. Back
past one point six two. Back past one point

682
01:10:35,674 --> 01:10:36,011
one.

683
01:10:36,463 --> 01:10:40,947
All the way back down to one — exactly where
it started.

684
01:10:47,129 --> 01:10:51,611
The cause has completely reversed. The effect
has not.

685
01:10:55,163 --> 01:10:58,912
Take an iron bar, and loop a wire around it.

686
01:10:59,329 --> 01:11:05,036
Pass an electric current through the wire, and
the iron becomes magnetic.

687
01:11:05,463 --> 01:11:07,790
Now switch the current off.

688
01:11:08,229 --> 01:11:15,547
The magnetism does not go away. Some of it
stays. The cause was temporary. The effect

689
01:11:15,547 --> 01:11:16,150
lasted.

690
01:11:19,696 --> 01:11:26,041
hysteresis. When the cause is reversed and the
effect stays behind.

691
01:11:31,263 --> 01:11:35,712
Push the money coming in far enough down, and
she quits for good.

692
01:11:36,129 --> 01:11:40,608
Now let it recover, all the way back to where
it started.

693
01:11:41,029 --> 01:11:47,450
She does not come back. Because coming back
means paying the whole sunk cost over again.

694
01:11:49,296 --> 01:11:56,329
And the paper's own careful point. Sunk costs
alone can produce some of this. Adding

695
01:11:56,329 --> 01:11:59,510
uncertainty magnifies it dramatically.

696
01:12:03,029 --> 01:12:09,843
The dollar rose, and foreign firms poured into
America — years late, because they were

697
01:12:09,843 --> 01:12:11,348
waiting to be sure.

698
01:12:11,796 --> 01:12:18,407
Then the dollar fell back to where it had
been. And they did not leave. They had paid to get

699
01:12:18,407 --> 01:12:22,791
in, and leaving would mean paying to get back
in again later.

700
01:12:24,629 --> 01:12:32,037
The cause reversed. The effect stayed. That is
all hysteresis is — and now you have watched

701
01:12:32,037 --> 01:12:33,340
it happen twice.

702
01:12:36,163 --> 01:12:42,612
up past 1.10, nothing · past 1.62, she builds

703
01:12:42,729 --> 01:12:47,405
back to 1.00 — and she does not close

704
01:12:47,529 --> 01:12:49,672
the bar stays magnetic

705
01:12:49,796 --> 01:12:53,842
hysteresis explains the dollar and the imports

706
01:13:02,229 --> 01:13:09,208
Here is Nell, in America. And here is Kenji,
running a similar factory in Japan.

707
01:13:09,629 --> 01:13:16,936
American firms of the period demanded very
high returns before they would build — and then

708
01:13:16,936 --> 01:13:24,406
abandoned whole fields after short stretches
of losses. Colour televisions. Video recorders.

709
01:13:24,406 --> 01:13:25,623
Semiconductors.

710
01:13:26,063 --> 01:13:33,356
Japanese firms did the opposite on both
counts. They invested aggressively, and they hung

711
01:13:33,356 --> 01:13:33,601
on.

712
01:13:38,729 --> 01:13:45,413
Remember what uncertainty does to the two
lines. It raises the build-line and lowers the

713
01:13:45,413 --> 01:13:49,667
quit-line. Together. It cannot do one without
the other.

714
01:13:51,496 --> 01:13:58,654
So a firm too hesitant to invest should be
more willing to ride out bad periods, not less.

715
01:13:58,654 --> 01:14:04,698
The American firms were the opposite on both
counts. The story does not fit.

716
01:14:08,229 --> 01:14:15,380
The usual explanation is lifetime employment,
which makes labour a cost you carry whether

717
01:14:15,380 --> 01:14:16,827
you use it or not.

718
01:14:17,263 --> 01:14:24,531
Lower day-to-day costs do mean revenue has to
fall further before quitting makes sense. So

719
01:14:24,531 --> 01:14:25,500
far so good.

720
01:14:25,929 --> 01:14:32,949
But larger fixed and sunk commitments should
make those same firms reluctant investors.

721
01:14:33,396 --> 01:14:40,324
And they were the opposite. Particularly
aggressive ones. So that explanation does not work

722
01:14:40,324 --> 01:14:40,857
either.

723
01:14:44,396 --> 01:14:51,268
Here is the spread of possible futures for a
firm. Bad ones to the left, good ones to the

724
01:14:51,268 --> 01:14:51,732
right.

725
01:14:52,163 --> 01:14:58,722
For the Japanese firms, the bad half was
cushioned. Government support, and cartels

726
01:14:58,722 --> 01:15:02,831
tolerated in recessions, cut off the worst
outcomes.

727
01:15:04,663 --> 01:15:11,701
Now use the bad news principle. Waiting is
worth less when there is less bad news to wait

728
01:15:11,701 --> 01:15:13,837
out. So they entered early.

729
01:15:17,663 --> 01:15:23,325
the good news principle. Staying is governed
by the good possible outcomes.

730
01:15:26,863 --> 01:15:33,688
And because the upside mattered relatively
more for them, they stayed late as well. One

731
01:15:33,688 --> 01:15:37,062
lopsided distribution, both puzzles solved.

732
01:15:40,596 --> 01:15:47,426
Which gives two rules, and they are probably
the most practically useful sentences in the

733
01:15:47,426 --> 01:15:52,874
whole article. To get firms to invest sooner,
reduce the downside risk.

734
01:15:54,696 --> 01:15:58,440
To stop firms leaving, improve the upside.

735
01:16:00,263 --> 01:16:07,054
Different instruments, for different jobs.
Remember that. Part Three is built on it.

736
01:16:09,896 --> 01:16:14,627
US: late in, early out · Japan: the reverse

737
01:16:14,763 --> 01:16:18,147
option value moves both lines together

738
01:16:18,296 --> 01:16:21,995
cushioned downside ⇒ early in, late out

739
01:16:22,129 --> 01:16:26,477
downside → entry · upside → staying

740
01:16:35,862 --> 01:16:40,547
Six similar firms, all facing the same
swinging demand.

741
01:16:40,996 --> 01:16:48,045
When the price rises to the entry line, new
firms come in. More supply, and the price stops

742
01:16:48,045 --> 01:16:51,221
rising. So it never gets above that line.

743
01:16:51,662 --> 01:16:58,464
And when the price falls to the exit line,
firms leave. Less supply, and the price stops

744
01:16:58,464 --> 01:17:02,174
falling. So it never gets below that one
either.

745
01:17:05,196 --> 01:17:12,298
Why must the entry line sit above average
cost? Suppose it did not — suppose entry capped

746
01:17:12,298 --> 01:17:15,011
the price exactly at average cost.

747
01:17:15,429 --> 01:17:22,296
Then firms could never do better than normal,
and bad spells would still push them below it.

748
01:17:22,729 --> 01:17:27,762
So on average they would lose. And nobody
would enter at all.

749
01:17:28,196 --> 01:17:35,115
Which means the entry line has to leave room
for the good spells to pay for the bad ones.

750
01:17:35,115 --> 01:17:40,713
The same argument upside down holds the exit
line below day-to-day cost.

751
01:17:44,262 --> 01:17:51,121
With these particular assumptions, the
market's two lines turn out to be exactly the ones a

752
01:17:51,121 --> 01:17:57,829
single firm on its own would have chosen. So
every number from chapter twenty-one carries

753
01:17:57,829 --> 01:17:58,884
straight over.

754
01:18:00,729 --> 01:18:07,373
Carry the caveat, because it matters later.
That result holds for identical, small,

755
01:18:07,373 --> 01:18:14,416
price-taking firms, under the paper's
particular assumptions. It is not a general truth.

756
01:18:17,962 --> 01:18:24,114
So what should we expect to see? In the
paper's own words: significant periods of

757
01:18:24,114 --> 01:18:29,885
supernormal profits with no new entry, and of
operating losses without exit.

758
01:18:31,729 --> 01:18:39,323
An industry sitting perfectly still.
Profitable firms not expanding. Loss-making firms not

759
01:18:39,323 --> 01:18:42,361
closing. And nothing wrong anywhere.

760
01:18:45,896 --> 01:18:52,726
A snapshot misleads. Firms making good profits
with no new entrants is not proof of

761
01:18:52,726 --> 01:18:53,467
monopoly.

762
01:18:53,896 --> 01:18:59,369
Firms selling below day-to-day cost is not
proof of predatory pricing.

763
01:18:59,796 --> 01:19:06,784
And there is a sting. An action aimed at those
profits — an antitrust case, a price cap —

764
01:19:06,784 --> 01:19:13,538
depresses entry. And the reduced supply can
actually raise the long-run average price.

765
01:19:13,962 --> 01:19:21,148
And a government that props firms up in bad
times will be anticipated. That draws in extra

766
01:19:21,148 --> 01:19:26,817
entry, which makes the losses worse when the
bad times actually arrive.

767
01:19:29,662 --> 01:19:33,685
entry caps at H · exit floors at L

768
01:19:33,829 --> 01:19:37,689
H above average cost, or nobody enters

769
01:19:37,829 --> 01:19:41,326
profits without entry · losses without exit

770
01:19:41,462 --> 01:19:43,628
so a snapshot misleads

771
01:19:52,029 --> 01:19:59,023
The paper closes with an observation that
reaches well outside business. Many personal,

772
01:19:59,023 --> 01:20:06,419
social and political decisions have exactly
the same three features. Costly to reverse. Made

773
01:20:06,419 --> 01:20:10,679
under uncertainty. And the timing is yours to
choose.

774
01:20:11,129 --> 01:20:14,386
So the same inertia applies to them too.

775
01:20:17,929 --> 01:20:24,369
A man and his partner, in New York, each with
a rent-controlled flat. Their relationship

776
01:20:24,369 --> 01:20:28,906
reaches the point where she suggests they give
one of them up.

777
01:20:29,329 --> 01:20:31,158
Let us give one of them up.

778
01:20:31,596 --> 01:20:38,450
And he explains to her, at length, the
importance of keeping options alive. It is unlikely

779
01:20:38,450 --> 01:20:44,086
they would split up, he says, but given a
positive probability, and so on.

780
01:20:45,929 --> 01:20:50,032
She took it very badly, and ended the
relationship.

781
01:20:53,562 --> 01:21:00,285
Financial economists who hear that story say
it proves how right he was. The paper says

782
01:21:00,285 --> 01:21:07,393
something better. He had misread the situation
entirely. It was not a decision problem under

783
01:21:07,393 --> 01:21:08,861
uncertainty at all.

784
01:21:12,396 --> 01:21:18,427
signalling. Doing something costly, because
the cost is what makes it believable.

785
01:21:22,529 --> 01:21:25,681
She was unsure how much he valued her.

786
01:21:26,129 --> 01:21:32,754
And it was precisely his willingness to take
the costly, irreversible step of giving up the

787
01:21:32,754 --> 01:21:39,378
flat that would have carried the message.
Anyone can say it. Only someone who means it will

788
01:21:39,378 --> 01:21:40,179
pay for it.

789
01:21:40,596 --> 01:21:44,688
He tried to sit on the fence, and fell off it.

790
01:21:47,529 --> 01:21:54,334
And that gives the chapter a real point rather
than a joke. It shows a limit of the whole

791
01:21:54,334 --> 01:22:01,214
framework. Option value is not always the
right lens. Sometimes the irreversibility is the

792
01:22:01,214 --> 01:22:01,826
message.

793
01:22:04,662 --> 01:22:07,683
the same three features appear elsewhere

794
01:22:07,829 --> 01:22:11,562
keeping the option open can be the wrong move

795
01:22:11,696 --> 01:22:14,581
signalling: the cost is the message

796
01:22:23,295 --> 01:22:26,730
investment: spend now, earn later

797
01:22:26,928 --> 01:22:29,779
some of it is sunk — never got back

798
01:22:29,995 --> 01:22:32,880
the future arrives a bit at a time

799
01:22:33,095 --> 01:22:35,307
the chance usually keeps

800
01:22:36,695 --> 01:22:41,043
so waiting pays: bad half cut, good half kept

801
01:22:41,262 --> 01:22:43,787
not about disliking risk

802
01:22:43,995 --> 01:22:47,554
waiting costs profit ⇒ a level to act at

803
01:22:47,762 --> 01:22:50,867
the trigger sits above the textbook line

804
01:22:52,262 --> 01:22:55,495
how far: choppiness and the cost of money

805
01:22:55,695 --> 01:23:00,531
base case ≈ double · rough trade > 3×

806
01:23:00,728 --> 01:23:02,824
cheaper money RAISES it

807
01:23:03,028 --> 01:23:06,761
in reverse: absorb losses before quitting

808
01:23:08,162 --> 01:23:12,312
the do-nothing band ≈ 9× wider

809
01:23:12,528 --> 01:23:15,602
up and back down — the effect stays

810
01:23:15,795 --> 01:23:18,541
cushion the downside ⇒ early in

811
01:23:18,728 --> 01:23:22,508
many firms ⇒ an industry that looks frozen

812
01:23:27,562 --> 01:23:34,198
Give up at nought point seven two. Build at
one point six two. And in between, a wide

813
01:23:34,198 --> 01:23:37,867
stretch where the right thing to do is
nothing.

814
01:23:41,395 --> 01:23:46,048
It does not say firms are irrational. It says
the opposite.

815
01:23:46,495 --> 01:23:49,977
It does not say waiting is always right.

816
01:23:50,395 --> 01:23:55,900
And it does not apply where a rival can snatch
the opportunity away from you.

817
01:24:00,028 --> 01:24:06,515
The farmer keeps going because closing is
final. Staying open keeps the good years possible,

818
01:24:06,515 --> 01:24:10,887
and that is worth more to him than the losses
are costing him.

819
01:24:12,728 --> 01:24:19,455
And I do not build, because building is final
too. Waiting another year is worth something,

820
01:24:19,455 --> 01:24:24,038
and this year's numbers are not yet good
enough to pay for it.

821
01:24:26,862 --> 01:24:33,417
One more thing before we stop. A government
somewhere spent hundreds of billions of pounds

822
01:24:33,417 --> 01:24:36,840
on a policy that assumed none of this was
true.

823
01:24:41,762 --> 01:24:43,927
the whole ladder, in order

824
01:24:44,062 --> 01:24:46,738
not irrational · not always right

825
01:24:46,862 --> 01:24:50,165
next: a policy that assumed none of it

826
01:25:06,083 --> 01:25:12,891
Nothing here assumes you know what a central
bank is, or an interest rate, or a bond. We

827
01:25:12,891 --> 01:25:18,229
build all of them from scratch, exactly as we
built cost and revenue.

828
01:25:18,649 --> 01:25:25,936
And there is one new person. This is Kit. He
is a student, and the argument in Part Three is

829
01:25:25,936 --> 01:25:33,223
his. He notices things, gets excited, and then
talks himself down when the evidence will not

830
01:25:33,223 --> 01:25:36,788
carry him. That last part is the whole point.

831
01:25:39,016 --> 01:25:44,279
central bank. The public body that looks after
a country's money.

832
01:25:49,083 --> 01:25:53,756
interest. The extra a borrower pays for the
use of money.

833
01:25:58,449 --> 01:26:05,758
Borrow a hundred pounds at five per cent, and
you pay back a hundred and five after a year.

834
01:26:09,283 --> 01:26:14,246
interest rate. How much extra, as a percentage
per year.

835
01:26:18,949 --> 01:26:24,857
monetary policy. What a central bank does to
make borrowing easier or harder.

836
01:26:29,383 --> 01:26:36,241
Meet the Governor. He runs the central bank.
He is well-meaning, he holds the levers, and he

837
01:26:36,241 --> 01:26:42,279
is genuinely trying to help. He is not the
villain of this film. There isn't one.

838
01:26:43,816 --> 01:26:51,038
After the crash of two thousand and eight, the
Bank wanted people and businesses to spend

839
01:26:51,038 --> 01:26:55,420
and build. So it did the usual thing. It cut
the rate.

840
01:26:55,849 --> 01:26:58,778
And it cut it again. And again.

841
01:26:59,216 --> 01:27:06,513
And by two thousand and nine the rate was
almost at zero. The lever had hit the floor. You

842
01:27:06,513 --> 01:27:08,945
cannot cut much below nothing.

843
01:27:10,783 --> 01:27:17,398
So they needed something else. That something
else is the subject of the next two chapters.

844
01:27:20,216 --> 01:27:23,264
central bank: looks after the money

845
01:27:23,383 --> 01:27:26,339
interest, and the rate, per year

846
01:27:26,483 --> 01:27:29,065
monetary policy: the lever

847
01:27:29,183 --> 01:27:32,939
by 2009 the lever hit the floor

848
01:27:42,349 --> 01:27:49,703
In normal times the Bank's rate travels down
this line, through a bank, and comes out as the

849
01:27:49,703 --> 01:27:51,861
rate an ordinary firm pays.

850
01:27:52,283 --> 01:27:59,225
Then, in two thousand and seven and eight,
banks stopped trusting each other. Lenders would

851
01:27:59,225 --> 01:28:04,108
provide funds to a bank only at a much higher
price — if at all.

852
01:28:04,549 --> 01:28:12,155
A wedge opened up. The Bank's rate went down.
The rate a firm actually paid did not follow

853
01:28:12,155 --> 01:28:12,409
it.

854
01:28:14,549 --> 01:28:20,046
credit spread. The wedge between the Bank's
rate and the rate a firm pays.

855
01:28:26,283 --> 01:28:33,462
On the eighth of October two thousand and
eight, central banks in Canada, China, the euro

856
01:28:33,462 --> 01:28:40,884
area, Sweden, Switzerland, the United Kingdom
and the United States all cut on the same day.

857
01:28:41,316 --> 01:28:47,688
In Britain, the Bank cut its rate by three
percentage points in the last three months of

858
01:28:47,688 --> 01:28:48,412
that year.

859
01:28:48,849 --> 01:28:55,733
And by a further one and a half percentage
points in the first three months of two thousand

860
01:28:55,733 --> 01:28:56,413
and nine.

861
01:28:56,849 --> 01:29:01,023
And there it stopped, at nought point five per
cent.

862
01:29:02,849 --> 01:29:08,979
And note that they stopped just above zero,
not at it — because of worries about what

863
01:29:08,979 --> 01:29:15,541
extremely low rates would do to banks' own
profits, and to the working of the money markets

864
01:29:15,541 --> 01:29:16,335
themselves.

865
01:29:20,283 --> 01:29:26,184
the zero lower bound. The point where a
central bank cannot cut any further.

866
01:29:30,983 --> 01:29:37,013
They could lend to banks that were short of
cash — the oldest job a central bank has.

867
01:29:37,449 --> 01:29:44,345
They could buy assets with money they created.
That is the one this film is about, and the

868
01:29:44,345 --> 01:29:46,107
next chapter builds it.

869
01:29:46,549 --> 01:29:52,787
Or they could simply say something about what
they intended to do in future — which turns

870
01:29:52,787 --> 01:29:55,591
out to matter more than you would think.

871
01:29:56,016 --> 01:30:01,768
All of that together goes by one name.
Unconventional monetary policy.

872
01:30:04,616 --> 01:30:08,024
lenders would fund banks only dearly

873
01:30:08,149 --> 01:30:10,275
a credit spread opened up

874
01:30:10,416 --> 01:30:16,027
cut 3 points, then 1.5 — stop at 0.5

875
01:30:16,149 --> 01:30:19,499
what is left: unconventional policy

876
01:30:27,883 --> 01:30:34,332
This is the chapter where it would be easiest
to lose you, so we go slowly, and we do the

877
01:30:34,332 --> 01:30:35,781
key sum three times.

878
01:30:36,216 --> 01:30:43,350
A government wants to spend more this year
than it collects in tax. So it needs to borrow.

879
01:30:45,183 --> 01:30:51,848
And here is somebody with money they would
like to put somewhere safe — a pension fund, an

880
01:30:51,848 --> 01:30:53,107
insurer, a saver.

881
01:30:53,549 --> 01:30:56,871
The saver hands over a hundred pounds.

882
01:30:59,516 --> 01:31:04,239
a gilt. A piece of British government debt,
bought and sold.

883
01:31:07,583 --> 01:31:14,627
And gets back a piece of paper. A promise.
Five pounds every year, and then the hundred

884
01:31:14,627 --> 01:31:16,894
pounds back on a fixed date.

885
01:31:19,916 --> 01:31:27,136
Year after year, the five pounds arrives. It
never changes. That is the whole point of it.

886
01:31:27,583 --> 01:31:32,405
And on the date printed on it, the hundred
pounds comes back.

887
01:31:36,649 --> 01:31:43,185
The saver does not have to keep it until then.
There is a market, and the gilt can be sold

888
01:31:43,185 --> 01:31:47,615
on to somebody else, at whatever price the two
of them agree.

889
01:31:48,049 --> 01:31:54,782
But whatever price it changes hands at, the
five pounds a year printed on it never changes.

890
01:31:54,782 --> 01:31:58,259
That one fact is the whole of the next
section.

891
01:32:03,483 --> 01:32:09,839
yield. What the fixed payments are worth, as a
percentage of today's price.

892
01:32:13,383 --> 01:32:20,273
The payments printed on the ticket never
change. Whatever happens, it pays five pounds a

893
01:32:20,273 --> 01:32:21,995
year. Hold on to that.

894
01:32:22,416 --> 01:32:29,699
Pay a hundred and twenty-five for exactly the
same ticket, and the same five pounds is only

895
01:32:29,699 --> 01:32:30,820
four per cent.

896
01:32:31,249 --> 01:32:37,915
Pay two hundred, and it is two and a half.
Same ticket. Same five pounds.

897
01:32:39,749 --> 01:32:47,111
Price up, return down. Always. There is no way
round it, because the payments on the ticket

898
01:32:47,111 --> 01:32:47,920
are fixed.

899
01:32:52,649 --> 01:32:55,790
The central bank creates new money.

900
01:32:56,216 --> 01:33:01,223
And uses it to buy these tickets. In enormous
quantities.

901
01:33:01,649 --> 01:33:08,911
So many buyers appear that the price of gilts
rises. And therefore, by the sum we just did

902
01:33:08,911 --> 01:33:11,251
three times, the yield falls.

903
01:33:15,583 --> 01:33:22,637
One careful sentence about creating money,
because it is the thing everyone misunderstands.

904
01:33:22,637 --> 01:33:29,535
The Bank credits the seller's account with
money that did not exist before, and takes the

905
01:33:29,535 --> 01:33:30,853
bond in exchange.

906
01:33:31,283 --> 01:33:37,171
Nothing is printed, and nothing is given away.
An asset is swapped for money.

907
01:33:40,016 --> 01:33:43,842
a gilt: fixed payments, on a fixed date

908
01:33:43,983 --> 01:33:47,797
price up → yield down, always

909
01:33:47,916 --> 01:33:51,544
the Bank creates money and buys gilts

910
01:33:51,683 --> 01:33:55,427
an asset swap — not a gift, not printing

911
01:34:03,816 --> 01:34:08,906
The policy is easy to describe and easy to get
wrong. So we are going to follow the money

912
01:34:08,906 --> 01:34:11,365
through three sets of books, one at a time.

913
01:34:13,483 --> 01:34:19,526
a balance sheet. What somebody owns on one
side, what they owe on the other.

914
01:34:24,749 --> 01:34:31,313
The people who actually sell the gilts are
usually not banks. They are pension funds and

915
01:34:31,313 --> 01:34:35,042
insurance companies — the non-bank private
sector.

916
01:34:39,983 --> 01:34:45,933
The seller hands over its gilts. So its
holdings of gilts go down.

917
01:34:46,383 --> 01:34:53,807
And it gets money instead — not printed notes,
but a number credited to its bank account. So

918
01:34:53,807 --> 01:34:55,340
its deposits go up.

919
01:34:57,183 --> 01:35:04,353
The central bank now owns the gilts. And it
pays for them by creating reserves — money that

920
01:35:04,353 --> 01:35:07,269
only banks hold, at the central bank.

921
01:35:09,416 --> 01:35:15,295
reserves. Money banks hold at the central
bank, and nobody else can.

922
01:35:18,383 --> 01:35:25,313
And the bank in the middle sits between them.
It has more reserves on one side, and it owes

923
01:35:25,313 --> 01:35:31,176
the seller more deposits on the other. Both
sides of its books grow together.

924
01:35:33,016 --> 01:35:39,756
Look at that and notice what has not happened.
Nobody has been given anything. Every single

925
01:35:39,756 --> 01:35:43,237
one of those entries is matched by another
one.

926
01:35:47,183 --> 01:35:53,942
What has happened is that the seller's
portfolio has been disturbed. It wanted gilts, and

927
01:35:53,942 --> 01:36:00,169
now it is holding money. And that disturbance
is where the whole mechanism starts.

928
01:36:03,716 --> 01:36:10,262
Ordinary monetary policy sets the price of
money. One short-term interest rate, and

929
01:36:10,262 --> 01:36:15,310
everything else follows from what people
expect that rate to do.

930
01:36:15,749 --> 01:36:23,056
Quantitative easing sets the quantity instead.
And it aims directly at longer-term rates,

931
01:36:23,056 --> 01:36:26,914
rather than reaching them through
expectations.

932
01:36:30,449 --> 01:36:36,860
And the authors are careful here. There is
nothing unusual about a central bank buying

933
01:36:36,860 --> 01:36:43,643
assets at all. What distinguishes these
operations is the circumstances they took place in,

934
01:36:43,643 --> 01:36:44,836
and their scale.

935
01:36:45,283 --> 01:36:51,570
And one more difference, which matters
enormously in the next chapter. What is bought.

936
01:36:51,570 --> 01:36:56,468
Short-dated government debt is very nearly the
same thing as money.

937
01:36:56,916 --> 01:37:03,744
Long-dated gilts, company debt and
mortgage-backed securities are not. The effectiveness of

938
01:37:03,744 --> 01:37:08,321
the policy may depend on what is bought, as
well as how much.

939
01:37:11,149 --> 01:37:14,847
the sellers are pension funds, not banks

940
01:37:14,983 --> 01:37:18,484
three sets of books, every entry matched

941
01:37:18,616 --> 01:37:21,838
QE sets a quantity, not a price

942
01:37:21,983 --> 01:37:25,727
and what is bought matters, not just how much

943
01:37:32,649 --> 01:37:39,382
Leg one of the chain has three channels, and
this is the big one. The one the Bank of

944
01:37:39,382 --> 01:37:41,442
England itself puts first.

945
01:37:43,483 --> 01:37:50,317
At the zero lower bound, money pays nothing.
And a piece of government debt that matures

946
01:37:50,317 --> 01:37:55,055
next month, at a rate of practically zero,
also pays nothing.

947
01:37:55,483 --> 01:38:01,782
If the seller does not care which of those two
it holds, the story ends right there. It

948
01:38:01,782 --> 01:38:06,198
takes the money, puts it in a drawer, and
nothing else moves.

949
01:38:10,216 --> 01:38:16,224
a liquidity trap. When extra money is simply
held, and nothing moves.

950
01:38:19,749 --> 01:38:26,835
Economists have a name for that. And buying
short-dated debt with money — what is sometimes

951
01:38:26,835 --> 01:38:33,842
called pure quantitative easing — can run
straight into it. Japan did exactly that between

952
01:38:33,842 --> 01:38:37,423
two thousand and one and two thousand and six.

953
01:38:40,949 --> 01:38:47,780
Line up the things somebody can hold, from the
safest at the bottom to the riskiest at the

954
01:38:47,780 --> 01:38:48,084
top.

955
01:38:48,516 --> 01:38:55,737
A ten-year gilt pays more than money. Sell it
and hold the money instead, and the return on

956
01:38:55,737 --> 01:38:57,879
your whole portfolio drops.

957
01:39:02,116 --> 01:39:08,130
The seller has money it did not want. So it
goes looking for something else to hold —

958
01:39:08,130 --> 01:39:12,233
something a little riskier, which is now
relatively cheap.

959
01:39:12,683 --> 01:39:18,702
And whoever sells it that thing now has the
money instead. So they go looking too.

960
01:39:19,149 --> 01:39:25,561
The authors call it passing the money around
like a hot potato. Nobody wants to be left

961
01:39:25,561 --> 01:39:26,372
holding it.

962
01:39:29,916 --> 01:39:36,774
Think of all the interest-rate risk in the
bond market as one pool, which somebody has to

963
01:39:36,774 --> 01:39:37,236
carry.

964
01:39:37,683 --> 01:39:44,826
When the central bank buys long-dated bonds,
it carries some of that risk itself. So there

965
01:39:44,826 --> 01:39:48,080
is less left for everybody else to carry.

966
01:39:48,516 --> 01:39:55,328
And so the extra return they demand for
carrying it falls, which pushes longer-term real

967
01:39:55,328 --> 01:40:02,373
interest rates down. That is the thinking
behind the American programme nicknamed operation

968
01:40:02,373 --> 01:40:02,838
twist.

969
01:40:05,683 --> 01:40:09,845
if money and bills are the same, nothing moves

970
01:40:09,983 --> 01:40:13,525
buy long, and the seller must go up the ladder

971
01:40:13,649 --> 01:40:16,920
money passed along like a hot potato

972
01:40:17,049 --> 01:40:21,293
and the Bank carries risk everyone else was
carrying

973
01:40:29,681 --> 01:40:36,158
The second channel does not need the money to
go anywhere at all. It works purely through

974
01:40:36,158 --> 01:40:38,487
what the action tells everybody.

975
01:40:38,914 --> 01:40:45,630
A very large purchase is a signal. It says the
central bank is serious about getting the

976
01:40:45,630 --> 01:40:50,743
economy going, and that it will keep rates low
for a long time yet.

977
01:40:51,181 --> 01:40:57,772
And if everybody believes that, they act on it
now, before anything else has happened at

978
01:40:57,772 --> 01:40:58,071
all.

979
01:40:59,914 --> 01:41:07,086
Which helps keep people's expectations of
future inflation anchored. And expectations, as a

980
01:41:07,086 --> 01:41:10,869
later chapter will show, are themselves a
lever.

981
01:41:16,114 --> 01:41:21,688
liquidity. How easily a thing can be sold
without moving its price.

982
01:41:25,014 --> 01:41:31,769
When markets seize up, you may not be able to
find a buyer at all. So investors demand a

983
01:41:31,769 --> 01:41:35,376
higher return to compensate them for that
risk.

984
01:41:37,814 --> 01:41:45,474
A central bank buying on a very large scale
is, among other things, a buyer. It puts trading

985
01:41:45,474 --> 01:41:49,638
back into the market, and that premium comes
down.

986
01:41:53,181 --> 01:41:59,184
But the authors are careful about this one.
The effect probably lasts only while the

987
01:41:59,184 --> 01:42:00,827
purchases are going on.

988
01:42:01,247 --> 01:42:06,194
And in gilt markets, which are normally very
liquid anyway,

989
01:42:06,614 --> 01:42:08,222
it may be small.

990
01:42:10,047 --> 01:42:16,857
There is also disagreement about how big those
effects were and how long they lasted. And

991
01:42:16,857 --> 01:42:22,749
the effects on wider classes of assets are
less marked than on company bonds.

992
01:42:25,581 --> 01:42:28,756
channel two works purely through the signal

993
01:42:28,881 --> 01:42:33,043
channel three puts trading back in a frozen
market

994
01:42:33,181 --> 01:42:36,298
and the authors hedge channel three hard

995
01:42:44,714 --> 01:42:51,888
Leg one moved the prices of things in
financial markets. Leg two has to turn that into

996
01:42:51,888 --> 01:42:56,977
somebody actually spending money. And it does
it in two ways.

997
01:43:00,514 --> 01:43:07,653
And that gives the sentence the whole policy
rests on. Their words: this fall in the cost of

998
01:43:07,653 --> 01:43:14,405
capital should boost consumption and
investment, by increasing incentives to borrow and

999
01:43:14,405 --> 01:43:16,578
reducing incentives to save.

1000
01:43:22,714 --> 01:43:28,223
Banks were badly damaged, so any route through
them was likely impaired.

1001
01:43:28,647 --> 01:43:34,377
Households and smaller companies have no
access to capital markets at all.

1002
01:43:34,814 --> 01:43:38,477
So they may not directly benefit from this
channel.

1003
01:43:44,814 --> 01:43:51,254
And be fair to them, because they do not leave
it there. They name three further routes by

1004
01:43:51,254 --> 01:43:53,973
which small firms might still benefit.

1005
01:43:57,514 --> 01:44:04,227
An obvious implication of a fall in gilt
yields is that the government's own cost of

1006
01:44:04,227 --> 01:44:08,863
borrowing is now lower. Gilts are exactly what
was bought.

1007
01:44:09,281 --> 01:44:15,550
And their answer is that it will not change
what the government does. Governments take a

1008
01:44:15,550 --> 01:44:22,033
longer-term view. Their spending plans should
therefore be unaffected by cyclical movements

1009
01:44:22,033 --> 01:44:23,316
in interest rates.

1010
01:44:26,847 --> 01:44:32,888
When asset prices go up, the people who own
those assets are richer. And higher wealth

1011
01:44:32,888 --> 01:44:34,714
should mean more spending.

1012
01:44:35,147 --> 01:44:41,653
They put a number on it. Three hundred and
seventy-five billion pounds of announced

1013
01:44:41,653 --> 01:44:48,863
purchases will eventually boost British
households' net financial wealth by about thirty per

1014
01:44:48,863 --> 01:44:49,255
cent.

1015
01:44:51,081 --> 01:44:57,824
Those gains went largely to the households
holding the most financial assets — in particular

1016
01:44:57,824 --> 01:45:04,421
older and more affluent ones. As with all
monetary policy, they say, there are winners and

1017
01:45:04,421 --> 01:45:04,934
losers.

1018
01:45:07,781 --> 01:45:11,102
cheaper borrowing, and owners feeling richer

1019
01:45:11,247 --> 01:45:15,572
the cost-of-capital sentence, hedged three
times

1020
01:45:15,714 --> 01:45:18,913
and three named ways round the hedge

1021
01:45:19,047 --> 01:45:27,285
£375bn → about +30% of net financial wealth

1022
01:45:34,214 --> 01:45:39,276
There is a second way of telling the same
story, and it clears up the thing almost everybody

1023
01:45:39,276 --> 01:45:42,467
gets wrong about this policy. So it is worth
four minutes.

1024
01:45:44,514 --> 01:45:51,824
Narrow money is reserves — the money banks
hold at the central bank. Nobody else can touch

1025
01:45:51,824 --> 01:45:52,067
it.

1026
01:45:52,514 --> 01:45:59,694
Broad money is deposits — the money in
everybody else's bank account. That is the one that

1027
01:45:59,694 --> 01:46:00,571
gets spent.

1028
01:46:02,414 --> 01:46:09,195
And the transmission of this policy runs
through broad money. Not narrow money. The Bank of

1029
01:46:09,195 --> 01:46:11,504
England has said so explicitly.

1030
01:46:15,547 --> 01:46:22,976
Put a pound of reserves into the system, says
the textbook, and broad money rises by ten or

1031
01:46:22,976 --> 01:46:24,772
fifteen times as much.

1032
01:46:25,214 --> 01:46:27,826
In Britain, it was less than one.

1033
01:46:30,847 --> 01:46:37,375
And the authors say plainly that this is not
evidence the policy failed. On their own

1034
01:46:37,375 --> 01:46:44,440
account, the right thing to expect was about
one, not fifteen. The failure of broad money to

1035
01:46:44,440 --> 01:46:47,973
explode is exactly what their theory predicts.

1036
01:46:51,514 --> 01:46:57,836
Broad money grew by less than two hundred
billion pounds during the first round.

1037
01:46:58,281 --> 01:47:02,431
Banks swapped short-term deposits for
long-term debt.

1038
01:47:02,881 --> 01:47:09,784
And large companies raised money by issuing
bonds instead of borrowing from banks. Both of

1039
01:47:09,784 --> 01:47:11,702
those reduce broad money.

1040
01:47:15,247 --> 01:47:21,361
You will hear it said that the banks are
sitting on all that money instead of lending it

1041
01:47:21,361 --> 01:47:21,639
out.

1042
01:47:22,081 --> 01:47:29,004
The banking system cannot do that. The amount
of reserves in the system is decided by how

1043
01:47:29,004 --> 01:47:35,071
the central bank chose to fund its purchases —
not by any bank's own decision.

1044
01:47:35,514 --> 01:47:42,898
Reserves went up a lot because of how the
purchases were paid for. That is arithmetic, not a

1045
01:47:42,898 --> 01:47:43,460
choice.

1046
01:47:46,981 --> 01:47:53,592
The only way more reserves can lead to more
lending is if they change a bank's incentive to

1047
01:47:53,592 --> 01:47:58,024
lend — for instance by making the bank's own
funding cheaper.

1048
01:47:58,447 --> 01:48:01,692
Which is a completely different mechanism.

1049
01:48:04,514 --> 01:48:08,932
narrow money is reserves; broad money is
deposits

1050
01:48:09,081 --> 01:48:11,803
the policy runs through broad money

1051
01:48:11,947 --> 01:48:15,348
the multiplier was under one — as predicted

1052
01:48:15,481 --> 01:48:19,132
banks cannot choose the quantity of reserves

1053
01:48:26,079 --> 01:48:30,779
The authors do something in this section that
a less honest article would have left out.

1054
01:48:30,779 --> 01:48:35,478
They set out, at length, a well-known result
which says the whole policy does nothing at

1055
01:48:35,478 --> 01:48:35,692
all.

1056
01:48:37,713 --> 01:48:44,792
The seller hands over something risky and gets
back something safe. Every channel so far has

1057
01:48:44,792 --> 01:48:49,870
assumed that changes how much risk the private
sector is carrying.

1058
01:48:53,413 --> 01:48:56,918
Start with the risk sitting in the private
sector.

1059
01:48:57,346 --> 01:49:03,017
It did not disappear. It moved onto the
central bank's own books.

1060
01:49:03,446 --> 01:49:07,002
And who owns the central bank? The government.

1061
01:49:07,446 --> 01:49:13,712
And who pays for the government? Households,
through the taxes they will pay in future.

1062
01:49:14,146 --> 01:49:20,836
And households are the private sector. The
risk has gone all the way round and come back to

1063
01:49:20,836 --> 01:49:22,673
exactly where it started.

1064
01:49:23,113 --> 01:49:29,772
So the household's risk, taken as a whole, has
not changed. There is nothing to rebalance.

1065
01:49:29,772 --> 01:49:32,361
And the policy does nothing at all.

1066
01:49:35,879 --> 01:49:40,027
People have to see straight through to the
state's own books.

1067
01:49:40,446 --> 01:49:44,492
There must be no limits on who can hold what.

1068
01:49:44,913 --> 01:49:49,377
And the future taxes must fall on the very
same people.

1069
01:49:49,813 --> 01:49:54,722
And the authors say it plainly. Those
assumptions are very strong.

1070
01:49:58,246 --> 01:50:04,604
There is one way out of it, and it is a
promise. The central bank says it will hold rates

1071
01:50:04,604 --> 01:50:09,819
low for longer than it otherwise would — even
once things have recovered.

1072
01:50:10,246 --> 01:50:16,687
And here is the catch, which the authors are
careful about. The promise has to be believed.

1073
01:50:17,113 --> 01:50:23,897
But once the economy has recovered — precisely
because everyone believed the promise — the

1074
01:50:23,897 --> 01:50:27,139
central bank has no reason left to keep it.

1075
01:50:29,479 --> 01:50:34,347
A promise nobody has to keep is a promise
nobody believes.

1076
01:50:37,879 --> 01:50:44,711
Which is why one reading of quantitative
easing is that its real job was the signalling all

1077
01:50:44,711 --> 01:50:50,567
along — an action, rather than a promise, and
actions are harder to take back.

1078
01:50:53,413 --> 01:50:57,285
risk moved to the state and back through taxes

1079
01:50:57,413 --> 01:51:00,669
so in that world the policy does nothing

1080
01:51:00,813 --> 01:51:03,977
the assumptions behind it are very strong

1081
01:51:04,113 --> 01:51:07,487
and the way out is a promise nobody must keep

1082
01:51:15,879 --> 01:51:22,401
Buying assets was not the only thing central
banks did. The authors group the rest into

1083
01:51:22,401 --> 01:51:29,223
three, and each one is worth knowing, because
Part Three has to be fair about what else was

1084
01:51:29,223 --> 01:51:29,898
going on.

1085
01:51:34,446 --> 01:51:41,562
Left to themselves, people expect a rate that
has been cut to nothing to start rising again

1086
01:51:41,562 --> 01:51:42,500
fairly soon.

1087
01:51:42,946 --> 01:51:49,734
So the central bank simply tells them
otherwise. It says the rate will stay where it is for

1088
01:51:49,734 --> 01:51:50,928
a long time yet.

1089
01:51:52,746 --> 01:51:59,068
And the authors are careful about how well it
worked. It did move expectations.

1090
01:51:59,512 --> 01:52:04,689
But markets never fully priced in the cuts the
announcements implied.

1091
01:52:05,112 --> 01:52:11,627
And none of it was quite the promise to be
irresponsible later that the theory in the last

1092
01:52:11,627 --> 01:52:13,582
chapter actually calls for.

1093
01:52:17,112 --> 01:52:24,324
This one is the oldest job a central bank has,
and it is not really monetary policy at all.

1094
01:52:24,324 --> 01:52:31,614
When a sound bank cannot borrow simply because
everyone has panicked, the central bank lends

1095
01:52:31,614 --> 01:52:35,814
to it — at a penal rate, and against good
collateral.

1096
01:52:36,246 --> 01:52:43,462
And in the euro area, two operations in late
twenty-eleven and early twenty-twelve lent over

1097
01:52:43,462 --> 01:52:50,130
a trillion euros for three years — aimed,
their central bank argued, at repairing the

1098
01:52:50,130 --> 01:52:52,483
transmission mechanism itself.

1099
01:52:56,012 --> 01:53:02,770
The third group exists because of the wedge
from an earlier chapter. Banks looked risky, so

1100
01:53:02,770 --> 01:53:08,859
their own funding was dear, so what they
charged households and firms stayed high.

1101
01:53:09,279 --> 01:53:16,218
The first was a guarantee. The government
stands behind up to twenty billion pounds of bank

1102
01:53:16,218 --> 01:53:23,157
debt, so an investor buying it faces the same
risk as buying a gilt. And the bank gets that

1103
01:53:23,157 --> 01:53:28,953
funding at a discount if it agrees to cut what
it charges smaller companies.

1104
01:53:29,379 --> 01:53:36,344
Though twenty billion is only about eight per
cent of a year's lending to small firms by the

1105
01:53:36,344 --> 01:53:40,507
largest banks. Limited in scale, as the
authors put it.

1106
01:53:44,046 --> 01:53:50,491
The second was bigger, and cleverer. Four-year
funding, of at least five per cent of a

1107
01:53:50,491 --> 01:53:56,712
bank's existing loan book — around eighty
billion pounds across the eligible banks.

1108
01:53:57,146 --> 01:54:03,630
And every extra pound a bank lent raised the
amount it could borrow under the scheme.

1109
01:54:04,079 --> 01:54:10,122
A bank holding its lending steady or growing
it paid a quarter of one per cent.

1110
01:54:10,546 --> 01:54:17,774
A bank shrinking its lending by five per cent
paid one and a half — six times as much, on

1111
01:54:17,774 --> 01:54:19,155
the whole amount.

1112
01:54:21,979 --> 01:54:26,501
guidance: the rate path, held flat by
announcement

1113
01:54:26,646 --> 01:54:30,768
lending in a panic: penal rate, good
collateral

1114
01:54:30,912 --> 01:54:34,088
and schemes aimed straight at the wedge

1115
01:54:34,212 --> 01:54:38,909
one of which charged 6× more for shrinking
lending

1116
01:54:47,312 --> 01:54:54,095
Two economists, writing in twenty-twelve, set
out how the policy is supposed to work.

1117
01:54:54,095 --> 01:54:56,888
Christopher Bowdler and Amar Radia.

1118
01:54:57,312 --> 01:55:03,795
They are careful, serious people, and nothing
in this film is an attack on them. Their

1119
01:55:03,795 --> 01:55:05,981
colour, from here on, is red.

1120
01:55:09,512 --> 01:55:16,405
And the chain is the picture Part Two is built
on, exactly as the vertical scale was Part

1121
01:55:16,405 --> 01:55:16,870
One's.

1122
01:55:17,312 --> 01:55:23,265
Why a chain? Because it is honest. Each link
can be examined on its own.

1123
01:55:23,712 --> 01:55:28,365
And if any single link fails, the whole thing
stops there.

1124
01:55:32,612 --> 01:55:39,344
The authors themselves split the chain into
two legs. From the purchases to the price of

1125
01:55:39,344 --> 01:55:39,880
assets.

1126
01:55:40,312 --> 01:55:45,508
And from the price of assets to what anybody
actually spends.

1127
01:55:47,346 --> 01:55:53,434
And Kit wants to say this out loud, now,
before anything else. He is only ever disputing the

1128
01:55:53,434 --> 01:55:55,155
second leg. Not the first.

1129
01:55:55,579 --> 01:56:00,870
The first leg is the part everybody argues
about, and he is not arguing about it.

1130
01:56:04,512 --> 01:56:10,091
And within that second leg, one link is going
to matter more than all the others.

1131
01:56:10,512 --> 01:56:17,040
Link five. The firm decides to build. Keep
your eye on it for the rest of the film.

1132
01:56:19,879 --> 01:56:24,169
Bowdler and Radia set the policy out as a
chain

1133
01:56:24,312 --> 01:56:28,881
leg one: purchases → the price of assets

1134
01:56:29,012 --> 01:56:33,488
leg two: prices → what anybody spends

1135
01:56:33,612 --> 01:56:37,043
and link five is where a firm decides

1136
01:56:52,967 --> 01:57:00,151
Here is the sentence the whole of Part Two
turns on. It is on page six hundred and twelve,

1137
01:57:00,151 --> 01:57:03,265
and these are the authors' exact words.

1138
01:57:05,100 --> 01:57:12,559
It contains two completely different kinds of
claim, stitched together. Split it in half.

1139
01:57:13,700 --> 01:57:20,719
The first half is a question about prices.
Does the cheaper money actually arrive at a firm?

1140
01:57:20,719 --> 01:57:22,702
You can go and check that.

1141
01:57:23,133 --> 01:57:29,530
The second half is a question about behaviour.
What does a firm do once the money has

1142
01:57:29,530 --> 01:57:33,670
arrived? That is a different sort of question
entirely.

1143
01:57:35,500 --> 01:57:39,198
The first half gets hedged. Banks were
impaired.

1144
01:57:39,633 --> 01:57:45,142
Households and smaller companies have no
access to capital markets.

1145
01:57:45,567 --> 01:57:48,390
So they may not directly benefit.

1146
01:57:48,833 --> 01:57:55,437
And be fair to them. These are honest, careful
qualifications. They also name several other

1147
01:57:55,437 --> 01:58:01,968
routes by which small firms might still
benefit — through supply chains, through a cheaper

1148
01:58:01,968 --> 01:58:08,571
currency helping exporters, and through banks
passing on lower rates. All of that is in the

1149
01:58:08,571 --> 01:58:09,151
article.

1150
01:58:09,600 --> 01:58:13,193
Now Kit waits for the same thing to happen to
the other half.

1151
01:58:17,033 --> 01:58:24,131
One half of that sentence is hedged three
times. The other half is not hedged once.

1152
01:58:27,667 --> 01:58:33,262
His first reaction is not disagreement, and
that matters for everything that follows.

1153
01:58:33,700 --> 01:58:38,455
His first reaction is that two halves of one
sentence are not the same kind of statement,

1154
01:58:38,455 --> 01:58:40,859
and are being given the same amount of trust.

1155
01:58:44,400 --> 01:58:51,986
This is not carelessness. It is what a certain
kind of economics does by construction. It

1156
01:58:51,986 --> 01:58:59,657
models markets in enormous detail, and it
treats a firm as a machine. Numbers in. Decision

1157
01:58:59,657 --> 01:58:59,998
out.

1158
01:59:00,433 --> 01:59:07,502
There is nothing dishonest in that. It is a
simplification, and simplifications are how

1159
01:59:07,502 --> 01:59:10,833
anyone thinks about anything complicated.

1160
01:59:12,667 --> 01:59:18,782
But it does mean that the last link in the
chain is the only one nobody examines.

1161
01:59:21,600 --> 01:59:25,275
one sentence, two different kinds of claim

1162
01:59:25,400 --> 01:59:28,761
the price half is hedged three times

1163
01:59:28,900 --> 01:59:32,064
the behaviour half is not hedged once

1164
01:59:32,200 --> 01:59:35,399
because a firm is modelled as a machine

1165
01:59:43,799 --> 01:59:50,072
A page later, the authors ask a question about
somebody else. What does the government do

1166
01:59:50,072 --> 01:59:52,609
when its own borrowing gets cheaper?

1167
01:59:53,033 --> 01:59:59,457
And notice what they open with. The
government's borrowing cost is now lower. Gilts are

1168
01:59:59,457 --> 02:00:06,030
exactly what the Bank had been buying. So if
anyone got the cheaper price, the government

1169
02:00:06,030 --> 02:00:06,325
did.

1170
02:00:06,766 --> 02:00:09,361
The money definitely arrives.

1171
02:00:11,199 --> 02:00:13,205
And nothing happens.

1172
02:00:18,333 --> 02:00:25,225
Here is what the authors say, in their own
words. Their spending plans should therefore be

1173
02:00:25,225 --> 02:00:29,130
unaffected by cyclical movements in interest
rates.

1174
02:00:31,366 --> 02:00:37,252
And look at the reason. It has nothing to do
with access. The money arrives.

1175
02:00:37,699 --> 02:00:44,611
The reason is about the horizon the decision
is taken over. Governments plan a long way

1176
02:00:44,611 --> 02:00:45,088
ahead.

1177
02:00:49,233 --> 02:00:54,581
Kit gets excited. They have admitted, he says,
that a feature of how the decision is made

1178
02:00:54,581 --> 02:00:56,143
can break the whole chain.

1179
02:00:57,966 --> 02:01:03,088
And then he reads it again, and crosses his
own conclusion out. That is the first of several

1180
02:01:03,088 --> 02:01:04,313
times he will do that.

1181
02:01:06,633 --> 02:01:13,509
What survives is this, and it is thinner than
he first thought. A feature of the decision

1182
02:01:13,509 --> 02:01:15,363
itself is allowed, once,

1183
02:01:15,799 --> 02:01:20,065
to break the link between a cheaper price and
more spending.

1184
02:01:20,499 --> 02:01:27,285
And nobody then asks whether anything else
about how a decision gets made could do the same.

1185
02:01:30,133 --> 02:01:35,851
That is a reason to go and look. It is not
evidence about what you find.

1186
02:01:38,499 --> 02:01:45,166
That sentence is the intellectual standard the
rest of the film is held to. Everything Kit

1187
02:01:45,166 --> 02:01:47,389
says from here has to meet it.

1188
02:01:50,233 --> 02:01:53,803
the government gets the cheaper money for
certain

1189
02:01:53,933 --> 02:01:57,480
and its plans are unaffected — their words

1190
02:01:57,599 --> 02:02:00,944
the reason is the horizon, not access

1191
02:02:01,066 --> 02:02:04,566
which is a reason to look, not a finding

1192
02:02:12,966 --> 02:02:20,653
Kit puts the two articles side by side, and
notices they are arguing about the same quantity

1193
02:02:20,653 --> 02:02:22,324
from opposite sides.

1194
02:02:22,766 --> 02:02:27,959
One of them asserts that a lower cost of
capital raises investment.

1195
02:02:28,399 --> 02:02:34,383
And the other spends a whole article showing
that the number standing between the cost of

1196
02:02:34,383 --> 02:02:37,879
capital and the decision is not the cost of
capital.

1197
02:02:41,399 --> 02:02:46,374
The bar a project must clear is the textbook
bar, multiplied.

1198
02:02:46,799 --> 02:02:51,518
And the multiplier grows as the world becomes
more uncertain.

1199
02:02:54,366 --> 02:03:00,720
Which means we can go back to the sheet from
chapter eight, and watch what the policy

1200
02:03:00,720 --> 02:03:02,963
actually does to a firm's bar.

1201
02:03:09,799 --> 02:03:17,403
Here is a firm in ordinary times. Twenty per
cent choppiness, money at five per cent. Its

1202
02:03:17,403 --> 02:03:21,162
bar is one point eight six times break-even.

1203
02:03:22,999 --> 02:03:30,231
The policy makes money cheaper. So the firm
slides this way — and the sheet under it goes

1204
02:03:30,231 --> 02:03:32,831
up, not down. Two point six one.

1205
02:03:34,666 --> 02:03:41,268
But the policy is only ever used in a crisis.
So at the very same moment, the firm is

1206
02:03:41,268 --> 02:03:46,317
dragged the other way too — into the choppiest
part of the sheet.

1207
02:03:48,166 --> 02:03:53,868
Both movements push the bar up. Not one up and
one down. Both up.

1208
02:03:56,699 --> 02:04:03,605
Which gives the sentence this chapter exists
for. Quantitative easing is not a weaker

1209
02:04:03,605 --> 02:04:10,672
version of a rate cut. It is a rate cut
delivered in the one situation that blunts rate

1210
02:04:10,672 --> 02:04:11,078
cuts.

1211
02:04:15,466 --> 02:04:23,231
And be clear about whose is whose. The bar,
and the fact that it rises with uncertainty, is

1212
02:04:23,231 --> 02:04:23,913
Dixit's.

1213
02:04:24,333 --> 02:04:27,385
Putting it next to this policy is Kit's.

1214
02:04:30,233 --> 02:04:34,453
one article asserts what the other spends
pages on

1215
02:04:34,599 --> 02:04:37,612
cheaper money moves the firm up the sheet

1216
02:04:37,733 --> 02:04:41,407
and a crisis moves it up the other way too

1217
02:04:41,533 --> 02:04:45,521
a rate cut delivered where rate cuts are
blunted

1218
02:04:53,932 --> 02:04:57,506
Cheap money is meant to make building
attractive.

1219
02:04:57,932 --> 02:05:01,216
But cheap money also makes waiting cheaper.

1220
02:05:01,665 --> 02:05:07,642
Because the cost of holding off for a year is
the return the money would have earned

1221
02:05:07,642 --> 02:05:12,053
meanwhile. Make that return small, and holding
off hurts less.

1222
02:05:16,099 --> 02:05:22,059
At a five per cent cost of capital, the
multiplier is one point eight six.

1223
02:05:22,499 --> 02:05:26,069
At two per cent, it is two point six one.

1224
02:05:29,599 --> 02:05:36,423
Think of a firm's bar as two pieces. The
break-even level, and the mark-up that waiting adds

1225
02:05:36,423 --> 02:05:37,388
on top of it.

1226
02:05:37,832 --> 02:05:40,740
The policy pushes the bottom piece down.

1227
02:05:41,165 --> 02:05:45,232
And at the same time it fattens the piece on
top.

1228
02:05:47,065 --> 02:05:53,584
The direction is not in doubt. The bar does
come down. The first effect wins.

1229
02:05:57,132 --> 02:06:01,901
What Kit originally wanted to say was sharper.
That it comes down by less than you would

1230
02:06:01,901 --> 02:06:02,280
expect.

1231
02:06:02,699 --> 02:06:07,593
He has withdrawn it. Because whether that is
true depends entirely on how you measure it.

1232
02:06:08,032 --> 02:06:14,637
Compare the two figures as percentage points,
and the bar falls by more than the cost of

1233
02:06:14,637 --> 02:06:15,462
money does.

1234
02:06:15,899 --> 02:06:20,350
Compare them as proportions, and it falls by
less.

1235
02:06:20,799 --> 02:06:25,460
Both sums are correct. Neither of them is the
true one. So he declines to pick.

1236
02:06:28,299 --> 02:06:34,943
The claim he is entitled to is the plain one.
Part of the policy is spent making patience

1237
02:06:34,943 --> 02:06:36,137
more attractive.

1238
02:06:40,765 --> 02:06:44,823
cheap money makes building and waiting both
cheaper

1239
02:06:44,965 --> 02:06:52,320
1.86 at 5% · 2.61 at 2% — Dixit's own figures

1240
02:06:52,465 --> 02:06:56,094
the bar does fall: the first effect wins

1241
02:06:56,232 --> 02:06:59,825
but part of the policy is spent on patience

1242
02:07:08,232 --> 02:07:15,195
Replay the bad news principle. When you can
wait, it is mainly the bad possible outcomes

1243
02:07:15,195 --> 02:07:17,886
that decide whether you build now.

1244
02:07:19,732 --> 02:07:24,646
So the question to ask is: what does this
policy actually compress?

1245
02:07:28,165 --> 02:07:35,138
In financial markets, there is a price for
carrying risk. Quantitative easing pushes that

1246
02:07:35,138 --> 02:07:37,566
price down. Squeeze the spring.

1247
02:07:41,099 --> 02:07:47,355
But the doubt inside a firm is a different
thing. Will anyone still be buying in three

1248
02:07:47,355 --> 02:07:50,410
years? Will this factory still make sense?

1249
02:07:50,832 --> 02:07:57,743
Those are different worries entirely. A
central bank buying government bonds reaches them

1250
02:07:57,743 --> 02:07:59,839
only indirectly, if at all.

1251
02:08:01,665 --> 02:08:06,630
So the honest version of his claim is about
how directly, not about whether.

1252
02:08:09,465 --> 02:08:15,490
What a central bank reaches directly is the
price at which risk trades.

1253
02:08:15,932 --> 02:08:20,721
What it reaches at one or two removes is the
doubt itself.

1254
02:08:23,565 --> 02:08:27,519
the bad half decides whether she builds now

1255
02:08:27,665 --> 02:08:31,328
the policy squeezes the price of risk directly

1256
02:08:31,465 --> 02:08:35,105
the doubt inside a firm, only indirectly

1257
02:08:35,232 --> 02:08:38,975
so the claim is about how directly, not
whether

1258
02:08:45,899 --> 02:08:51,037
This chapter exists because Kit's own source
damages him, and that is what intellectual

1259
02:08:51,037 --> 02:08:52,159
honesty looks like.

1260
02:08:54,199 --> 02:09:01,752
Replay the two countries. Japanese firms
invested boldly and hung on through losses, because

1261
02:09:01,752 --> 02:09:04,133
their downside was cushioned.

1262
02:09:10,365 --> 02:09:16,036
Which half of that spread does a policy that
props up prices and signals that the

1263
02:09:16,036 --> 02:09:18,696
authorities will act actually work on?

1264
02:09:19,132 --> 02:09:25,744
This half. Which is exactly the right half for
keeping a struggling firm from closing.

1265
02:09:29,265 --> 02:09:36,063
A policy that props up the upside is aimed at
exactly the right half of the distribution for

1266
02:09:36,063 --> 02:09:42,712
keeping firms alive. Which hands the policy a
mechanism for the very thing he was going to

1267
02:09:42,712 --> 02:09:44,190
explain another way.

1268
02:09:46,032 --> 02:09:50,610
Why include something that damages you?
Because it is what his own source says. The

1269
02:09:50,610 --> 02:09:51,933
discomfort is the point.

1270
02:09:54,765 --> 02:09:58,451
a cushioned downside keeps firms invested

1271
02:09:58,599 --> 02:10:01,484
the policy props up the good half

1272
02:10:01,632 --> 02:10:05,423
which is the right half for keeping firms
alive

1273
02:10:05,565 --> 02:10:09,194
so his own source hands the policy a mechanism

1274
02:10:17,598 --> 02:10:24,657
A third article, and a third pair of authors.
Christopher Martin and Costas Milas. Their job

1275
02:10:24,657 --> 02:10:31,486
was to gather up the studies of what the
policy actually achieved. Their colour is green.

1276
02:10:33,632 --> 02:10:40,618
Across the studies they gathered, quantitative
easing raised growth by somewhere between one

1277
02:10:40,618 --> 02:10:44,795
and three per cent, with a similar effect on
inflation.

1278
02:10:48,332 --> 02:10:55,301
And here is their verdict, in their own words.
Quantitative easing has proved effective in

1279
02:10:55,301 --> 02:11:02,347
limiting the scale of the downturn. However,
by itself, it is not strong enough to spark an

1280
02:11:02,347 --> 02:11:03,741
economic recovery.

1281
02:11:05,765 --> 02:11:12,944
And they hedge it themselves. This conclusion,
they say, is tentative. There is relatively

1282
02:11:12,944 --> 02:11:17,172
little evidence, and most of it uses the same
method.

1283
02:11:20,698 --> 02:11:28,306
In one line: effective at preventing a deeper
crisis, less effective at promoting recovery.

1284
02:11:29,732 --> 02:11:35,504
The first time Kit read that, he treated it as
a result. Here is what the numbers say. Write

1285
02:11:35,504 --> 02:11:36,570
it down, move on.

1286
02:11:36,998 --> 02:11:40,777
Reading it again, after Dixit, he cannot let
it alone.

1287
02:11:41,198 --> 02:11:46,485
Why would one policy be good at stopping a
fall and bad at starting a rise? Those are not

1288
02:11:46,485 --> 02:11:47,970
obviously different jobs.

1289
02:11:48,398 --> 02:11:53,360
And he is uncomfortable. Not with the article
— with himself, for having read that sentence

1290
02:11:53,360 --> 02:11:56,959
once already and never asked whether there was
a reason behind it.

1291
02:12:00,498 --> 02:12:06,928
Preventing a collapse needs markets that keep
working and the upside held up. And by the

1292
02:12:06,928 --> 02:12:13,505
good news principle, that is exactly what
stops a struggling firm closing. It is strongest

1293
02:12:13,505 --> 02:12:19,935
when things are frightening, because there is
most to repair and most upside to restore.

1294
02:12:20,365 --> 02:12:26,770
Starting a recovery needs firms to commit
money they cannot get back. By the bad news

1295
02:12:26,770 --> 02:12:33,626
principle, that turns on the downside — which
is exactly what fear makes them least willing

1296
02:12:33,626 --> 02:12:37,092
to face. It is strongest when things are calm.

1297
02:12:39,932 --> 02:12:47,461
So the policy is used in exactly the state
where the first job is easiest and the second is

1298
02:12:47,461 --> 02:12:48,123
hardest.

1299
02:12:48,565 --> 02:12:50,882
It steadies without starting.

1300
02:12:53,098 --> 02:12:59,795
And flag clearly which parts of that are Kit's
and not Dixit's. That propping up the upside

1301
02:12:59,795 --> 02:13:06,197
works best when things are frightening, and
that the two jobs therefore pull apart, are

1302
02:13:06,197 --> 02:13:10,612
steps Kit has added. Dixit's own text does not
contain them.

1303
02:13:13,432 --> 02:13:17,896
their verdict: prevents a crisis, less so a
recovery

1304
02:13:18,032 --> 02:13:21,648
the two jobs need opposite halves of the
spread

1305
02:13:21,765 --> 02:13:24,952
and opposite conditions to work in

1306
02:13:25,098 --> 02:13:29,621
it steadies without starting — Kit's step, not
Dixit's

1307
02:13:39,032 --> 02:13:46,466
You will recognise this picture instantly,
because it is the hysteresis one. Same scale,

1308
02:13:46,466 --> 02:13:50,183
same lines. Only the direction is different.

1309
02:13:50,632 --> 02:13:53,157
A firm that is already running.

1310
02:13:53,598 --> 02:13:57,436
The crisis arrives, and its takings fall.

1311
02:13:57,865 --> 02:14:04,591
Down past the level where the textbook would
have closed it. And still it holds on, because

1312
02:14:04,591 --> 02:14:07,991
holding on keeps the chance of recovery alive.

1313
02:14:08,432 --> 02:14:12,023
And then through the quit line. It closes.

1314
02:14:13,865 --> 02:14:20,593
Now the policy works. Conditions come back up
to normal. All the way back to where they

1315
02:14:20,593 --> 02:14:21,212
started.

1316
02:14:23,032 --> 02:14:29,712
The firm does not come back. Because coming
back means paying the whole set-up cost over

1317
02:14:29,712 --> 02:14:30,168
again.

1318
02:14:32,998 --> 02:14:39,041
State it plainly. What the policy stops from
happening does not need explaining.

1319
02:14:39,465 --> 02:14:46,483
What needs explaining is why, for the firms it
did not reach in time, nothing came back.

1320
02:14:50,932 --> 02:14:54,513
a firm absorbs losses, then closes

1321
02:14:54,632 --> 02:14:57,261
conditions recover all the way

1322
02:14:57,398 --> 02:15:00,040
and the firm does not come back

1323
02:15:00,165 --> 02:15:03,584
because coming back costs the set-up again

1324
02:15:10,531 --> 02:15:15,858
Four doors. Kit opens each one, and cannot
close it again. This is the chapter where the

1325
02:15:15,858 --> 02:15:19,672
film earns its credibility, so none of these
gets knocked down.

1326
02:15:22,098 --> 02:15:28,652
Door one. Firms may not have been short of
confidence at all. They may simply have been

1327
02:15:28,652 --> 02:15:35,130
unable to borrow. If the banks would not lend,
no bar inside the firm was ever tested.

1328
02:15:35,564 --> 02:15:42,080
Door two. They may have had no customers. If
nobody is buying, patience has nothing to do

1329
02:15:42,080 --> 02:15:48,522
with it. And which of the two does more of the
work is a question this argument does not

1330
02:15:48,522 --> 02:15:49,034
settle.

1331
02:15:49,464 --> 02:15:56,377
Door three. If several firms are racing for
the same opportunity, waiting is not possible,

1332
02:15:56,377 --> 02:15:59,141
and the textbook is right after all.

1333
02:15:59,564 --> 02:16:06,588
Door four. The policy did move prices in
financial markets. That is the best-established

1334
02:16:06,588 --> 02:16:11,936
finding in the whole body of research, and
nothing here touches it.

1335
02:16:13,764 --> 02:16:17,983
All four are left standing open. Kit does not
claim to have closed any of them.

1336
02:16:20,831 --> 02:16:28,081
Which fixes the scope of the whole argument.
It applies to investment a company can take its

1337
02:16:28,081 --> 02:16:30,051
time over. Not to a race.

1338
02:16:34,498 --> 02:16:37,685
firms may have been unable to borrow

1339
02:16:37,831 --> 02:16:40,588
or simply have had no customers

1340
02:16:40,731 --> 02:16:43,663
a race removes the option to wait

1341
02:16:43,798 --> 02:16:47,066
and the policy did move market prices

1342
02:16:55,464 --> 02:17:01,542
Grow Nell's factory into a large company, with
several separately run parts.

1343
02:17:03,364 --> 02:17:08,847
So say this explicitly, because it is easy to
miss. Kit's dispute is not about how high the

1344
02:17:08,847 --> 02:17:11,799
number is. It is about what kind of number it
is.

1345
02:17:15,331 --> 02:17:21,056
And here is the mechanism, as an actual queue
of projects at the boardroom door.

1346
02:17:21,498 --> 02:17:27,999
State the assumption openly, because it is
doing a lot of work and it might be wrong. In a

1347
02:17:27,999 --> 02:17:33,417
large company the thing that runs out first is
not money. It is management.

1348
02:17:35,264 --> 02:17:41,285
A minimum return set to solve that problem is
tied to the next project in the queue — the

1349
02:17:41,285 --> 02:17:46,087
best thing the company would have to drop in
order to take this one on.

1350
02:17:46,531 --> 02:17:52,980
And that is an internal quantity. It depends
on how many good ideas the business is

1351
02:17:52,980 --> 02:17:57,098
generating, and how many capable people it has
spare.

1352
02:18:00,631 --> 02:18:06,639
A fall in the return on government debt does
not lengthen anybody's working week.

1353
02:18:07,064 --> 02:18:10,828
The yield falls. And the queue does not move.

1354
02:18:14,364 --> 02:18:19,301
So here is the version he can defend. Not that
the number never moves. But that it moves on

1355
02:18:19,301 --> 02:18:22,068
the company's clock rather than the central
bank's.

1356
02:18:22,498 --> 02:18:27,782
Changing as the pipeline of ideas and the
stock of managers change, over years. For a policy

1357
02:18:27,782 --> 02:18:32,893
meant to work inside a downturn, that is close
enough to failure to matter. But it is not

1358
02:18:32,893 --> 02:18:35,478
the same as never, and he will not say never.

1359
02:18:38,998 --> 02:18:46,141
Treating a hurdle rate as a rationing device
rather than a calculation is not an idea Kit

1360
02:18:46,141 --> 02:18:46,864
invented.

1361
02:18:47,298 --> 02:18:50,554
What he would claim is the use he has put it
to.

1362
02:18:53,398 --> 02:18:57,467
a big company rations management, not money

1363
02:18:57,598 --> 02:19:01,644
so the bar is tied to the next project in the
queue

1364
02:19:01,764 --> 02:19:05,451
the yield falls, and the queue does not move

1365
02:19:05,598 --> 02:19:09,551
it moves on the company's clock, not the
Bank's

1366
02:19:17,964 --> 02:19:22,738
This is not a claim that quantitative easing
does not work.

1367
02:19:23,164 --> 02:19:29,029
Kit has not disputed that it moved prices in
financial markets, which is the

1368
02:19:29,029 --> 02:19:33,274
best-established finding in the whole body of
research.

1369
02:19:33,698 --> 02:19:37,001
It is not a claim that the theory is false.

1370
02:19:37,431 --> 02:19:42,842
It is a claim that the theory is incomplete,
in one specific place.

1371
02:19:47,564 --> 02:19:50,786
So rebuild the chain one final time.

1372
02:19:51,231 --> 02:19:56,101
Careful reasoning about what can go wrong at
every stage.

1373
02:19:56,531 --> 02:20:03,938
Except one. The stage where a quantity finally
has to change, because somebody in a room has

1374
02:20:03,938 --> 02:20:04,823
to say yes.

1375
02:20:09,064 --> 02:20:16,086
The first is Dixit's, and Kit is mostly
reporting it. When spending cannot be undone and the

1376
02:20:16,086 --> 02:20:22,650
future is unknown, the bar is higher than the
textbook says. And it rises as the world

1377
02:20:22,650 --> 02:20:24,558
becomes more frightening.

1378
02:20:24,998 --> 02:20:31,882
So a policy used in frightening times is
working against the largest version of that bar.

1379
02:20:31,882 --> 02:20:38,690
That is well-established economics, published
in a general economics journal, and simply

1380
02:20:38,690 --> 02:20:41,629
absent from the article he began with.

1381
02:20:42,064 --> 02:20:49,363
The second is Kit's. And he holds it loosely,
for all the reasons the four open doors gave.

1382
02:20:52,198 --> 02:20:56,070
The gap is not a mistake in anybody's
reasoning.

1383
02:20:56,498 --> 02:20:58,245
It is a boundary.

1384
02:21:01,664 --> 02:21:08,353
A model of the whole economy hands off to a
decision it does not model. And the study of how

1385
02:21:08,353 --> 02:21:14,897
that decision actually gets made sits in a
different journal, on a different reading list,

1386
02:21:14,897 --> 02:21:17,878
and in the other half of the same degree.

1387
02:21:21,998 --> 02:21:28,515
And the last shot is all of them, standing
either side of the chain, at the link nobody

1388
02:21:28,515 --> 02:21:29,189
examined.

1389
02:21:32,631 --> 02:21:36,921
not that the policy fails, or the theory is
false

1390
02:21:37,064 --> 02:21:40,611
that the theory is incomplete in one place

1391
02:21:40,731 --> 02:21:44,847
the bar rises exactly when the policy is used

1392
02:21:44,964 --> 02:21:48,918
and the last link is a boundary, not a mistake
